  {
    'subject': 'Biology',
    'chapterTitle': 'Characteristics and Classification of Living Things',
    'body': 'Biology is the study of life, and the very first thing a WASSCE candidate must be able to do is separate what is living from what is non-living, and then place living things into orderly groups. Living organisms are recognised by seven fundamental characteristics, often remembered with the mnemonic MR NIGER D: Movement, Respiration, Nutrition, Irritability (sensitivity), Growth, Excretion, Reproduction and Death. Movement in animals is usually locomotion of the whole body, while in plants it is limited to parts such as growing shoots bending toward light. Respiration is the release of energy from food inside cells, and it must not be confused with breathing, which is only gaseous exchange. Nutrition is the taking in and use of food materials; green plants are autotrophic and manufacture their own food by photosynthesis, while animals are heterotrophic and depend on ready made organic food. Irritability is the ability to detect and respond to stimuli such as light, heat, touch and chemicals. Growth is a permanent and irreversible increase in size, dry mass and complexity, and it differs from the accretion seen when a crystal enlarges. Excretion is the removal of the toxic waste products of metabolism, such as urea, carbon dioxide and excess water. Reproduction ensures continuity of the species, and death marks the end of the individual life span.\n\nBecause there are millions of species, biologists classify them so that study becomes manageable. Classification is the arrangement of organisms into groups based on shared characteristics, especially structural and evolutionary similarities. The taxonomic hierarchy runs Kingdom, Phylum (or Division in plants), Class, Order, Family, Genus, Species, and a useful memory aid is Kings Play Chess On Fine Grained Sand. A species is a group of organisms that can interbreed freely to produce fertile offspring. Carolus Linnaeus gave us binomial nomenclature, in which every organism receives a two part Latin name: the genus written with a capital letter and the species with a small letter, both italicised or underlined, for example Homo sapiens and Zea mays.\n\nThe five kingdom system of Whittaker recognises Monera (prokaryotes such as bacteria and blue green algae), Protista (single celled eukaryotes such as Amoeba, Paramecium, Euglena and Chlamydomonas), Fungi (non green saprophytes and parasites such as Rhizopus, Mucor and mushrooms), Plantae (multicellular photosynthetic organisms with cellulose walls) and Animalia (multicellular heterotrophs without cell walls). Within Plantae the main divisions are Thallophyta, Bryophyta, Pteridophyta and Spermatophyta, the last containing Gymnosperms and Angiosperms, which are further split into monocotyledons and dicotyledons. Within Animalia the important invertebrate phyla are Protozoa, Coelenterata, Platyhelminthes, Nematoda, Annelida, Arthropoda and Mollusca, while the phylum Chordata contains the vertebrate classes Pisces, Amphibia, Reptilia, Aves and Mammalia.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES AND EXAM STYLE QUESTIONS\n\nExample 1: Toluwani observed a specimen in the school laboratory that had a segmented body, three pairs of jointed legs, one pair of antennae and two pairs of wings. To which class of arthropods does the specimen belong and give two reasons.\nAnswer: The specimen belongs to the class Insecta. Reasons: it has three pairs of jointed legs, a body divided into head, thorax and abdomen, one pair of antennae and wings borne on the thorax.\n\nExample 2: Oyedele wrote the scientific name of maize as zea Mays. Identify two errors and rewrite the name correctly.\nAnswer: Errors are (i) the genus name zea was not given a capital letter and (ii) the species name Mays was wrongly capitalised; also the name was not italicised or underlined. Correct form: Zea mays.\n\nExample 3: State three features that distinguish a living organism from a moving motor vehicle produced by Zetra.\nAnswer: A living organism respires and releases energy from food in its cells, grows by increase in dry mass and complexity, reproduces to give new individuals of its kind, excretes metabolic wastes and eventually dies; the vehicle does none of these, it merely converts fuel by combustion and is repaired rather than healed.\n\nExample 4: Give the taxonomic hierarchy of the domestic cat from kingdom to species.\nAnswer: Kingdom Animalia, Phylum Chordata, Class Mammalia, Order Carnivora, Family Felidae, Genus Felis, Species Felis catus.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- Biology is the scientific study of living things.\n- The characteristics of life are movement, respiration, nutrition, irritability, growth, excretion, reproduction and death.\n- MR NIGER D is the standard mnemonic for these characteristics.\n- Respiration releases energy in cells; breathing only exchanges gases.\n- Growth is permanent, irreversible increase in size, dry mass and complexity.\n- Irritability is response to stimuli such as light, heat, touch and chemicals.\n- Excretion removes metabolic wastes such as urea and carbon dioxide.\n- Egestion is the removal of undigested food and is not excretion.\n- Classification places organisms in groups by shared characteristics.\n- The hierarchy is Kingdom, Phylum or Division, Class, Order, Family, Genus, Species.\n- A species is the smallest unit and members interbreed to give fertile offspring.\n- Linnaeus introduced binomial nomenclature.\n- Genus begins with a capital letter, species with a small letter; both are italicised or underlined.\n- Whittaker proposed the five kingdoms: Monera, Protista, Fungi, Plantae, Animalia.\n- Monera are prokaryotic and lack a true nucleus.\n- Protista are mainly unicellular eukaryotes such as Amoeba and Paramecium.\n- Fungi lack chlorophyll and feed saprophytically or parasitically.\n- Plant divisions: Thallophyta, Bryophyta, Pteridophyta, Spermatophyta.\n- Spermatophyta are seed plants and include Gymnosperms and Angiosperms.\n- Angiosperms are divided into monocotyledons and dicotyledons.\n- Monocots have one seed leaf, parallel venation and fibrous roots.\n- Dicots have two seed leaves, network venation and tap roots.\n- Invertebrate phyla include Protozoa, Coelenterata, Platyhelminthes, Nematoda, Annelida, Arthropoda and Mollusca.\n- Arthropods have jointed appendages and an exoskeleton of chitin.\n- Arthropod classes are Insecta, Arachnida, Crustacea and Myriapoda.\n- Chordates possess a notochord, dorsal hollow nerve cord and pharyngeal clefts.\n- Vertebrate classes are Pisces, Amphibia, Reptilia, Aves and Mammalia.\n- Mammals have hair, mammary glands, a diaphragm and are homoiothermic.\n- A dichotomous key uses paired contrasting statements to identify organisms.\n- Viruses are on the borderline of life since they reproduce only inside host cells.',
