  {
    'subject': 'Biology',
    'chapterTitle': 'Cell Structure and Organization',
    'body': 'The cell is the basic structural and functional unit of all living organisms. The cell theory, developed from the work of Robert Hooke, Schleiden, Schwann and Virchow, states that all living things are made of one or more cells, that the cell is the smallest unit of life, and that all cells arise from pre existing cells by division. Robert Hooke first used the word cell in 1665 after examining thin slices of cork under his microscope, and Anton van Leeuwenhoek later observed living single celled organisms.\n\nA typical cell is bounded by a cell membrane, also called the plasma membrane, which is a thin, living, differentially permeable layer made of phospholipid and protein. It controls the entry and exit of substances. Inside is the cytoplasm, a jelly like matrix in which the organelles are suspended and where many metabolic reactions occur. The nucleus is the control centre; it contains chromatin made of DNA and protein, it is bounded by a nuclear membrane with pores, and it contains one or more nucleoli that make ribosomes. Mitochondria are rod shaped organelles with folded inner membranes called cristae, and they are the sites of aerobic respiration, earning the name powerhouse of the cell. Ribosomes are tiny granules where protein synthesis occurs. The endoplasmic reticulum is a network of membranes; the rough type bears ribosomes and transports proteins, while the smooth type makes lipids. The Golgi apparatus packages and secretes materials. Lysosomes contain digestive enzymes that destroy worn out organelles and invading bacteria. Centrioles, found in animal cells, organise the spindle during cell division.\n\nPlant cells differ from animal cells in several examinable ways. A plant cell has a rigid cellulose cell wall outside the membrane, giving shape and mechanical support; an animal cell has none. A mature plant cell has one large central vacuole filled with cell sap that maintains turgidity; animal cells have small, temporary vacuoles if any. Plant cells contain plastids, including chloroplasts with chlorophyll for photosynthesis, chromoplasts for colour and leucoplasts for storage; animal cells have no plastids. Plant cells store carbohydrate as starch while animal cells store glycogen. Animal cells possess centrioles and are usually irregular in shape, whereas plant cells are regular and often rectangular.\n\nOrganisms show levels of organisation: cell, tissue, organ, system and organism. A tissue is a group of similar cells performing a common function, such as epithelial, muscular, nervous and connective tissue in animals, or epidermis, parenchyma, collenchyma, sclerenchyma, xylem and phloem in plants. An organ, such as the leaf or the heart, is made of several tissues, and a system is a group of organs working together. Complexity increases from single celled organisms such as Amoeba and Euglena, through colonies such as Volvox, to filamentous forms such as Spirogyra, and finally to fully multicellular plants and animals in which division of labour and cell differentiation occur.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES AND EXAM STYLE QUESTIONS\n\nExample 1: State four structural differences between a plant cell and an animal cell.\nAnswer: (i) Plant cell has a cellulose cell wall, animal cell has none. (ii) Plant cell has chloroplasts, animal cell has none. (iii) Plant cell has a large permanent central vacuole, animal cell has small or no vacuole. (iv) Animal cell has centrioles and an irregular shape, plant cell lacks centrioles and has a regular shape.\n\nExample 2: Oyedele placed a piece of fresh onion epidermis in strong salt solution and observed that the cytoplasm pulled away from the wall. Name the process and explain it.\nAnswer: The process is plasmolysis. The salt solution is hypertonic, so water leaves the cell sap by osmosis through the differentially permeable membrane; the vacuole shrinks and the protoplasm withdraws from the rigid cellulose wall.\n\nExample 3: Why are mitochondria more numerous in muscle cells and in sperm cells?\nAnswer: These cells are highly active and require large amounts of ATP for contraction and for swimming; mitochondria are the sites of aerobic respiration that produce ATP.\n\nExample 4: Toluwani viewed a cell that had no nuclear membrane and no mitochondria. To which kingdom does the organism most likely belong?\nAnswer: Kingdom Monera, because the cell is prokaryotic.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- The cell is the basic unit of structure and function in living things.\n- Robert Hooke coined the term cell after examining cork.\n- Cell theory: all organisms are made of cells, the cell is the smallest unit of life, and cells come from pre existing cells.\n- The cell membrane is living and differentially permeable.\n- The cell wall of plants is non living, made of cellulose and fully permeable.\n- Cytoplasm is the site of many metabolic reactions.\n- The nucleus controls cell activities and carries hereditary material.\n- Chromosomes are made of DNA and protein.\n- The nucleolus manufactures ribosomes.\n- Mitochondria carry out aerobic respiration and produce ATP.\n- Cristae increase the surface area inside mitochondria.\n- Ribosomes are the sites of protein synthesis.\n- Rough endoplasmic reticulum bears ribosomes; smooth type makes lipids.\n- The Golgi body packages and secretes substances.\n- Lysosomes contain hydrolytic enzymes and are called suicide bags.\n- Chloroplasts contain chlorophyll and trap light energy.\n- Plant cells store starch, animal cells store glycogen.\n- Centrioles occur in animal cells and organise the spindle.\n- Diffusion is movement of particles from high to low concentration.\n- Osmosis is movement of water through a differentially permeable membrane.\n- Active transport moves substances against a gradient using energy.\n- Plasmolysis occurs in hypertonic solutions; turgidity occurs in hypotonic solutions.\n- Haemolysis is the bursting of red blood cells in a hypotonic solution.\n- Levels of organisation: cell, tissue, organ, system, organism.\n- Animal tissues include epithelial, muscular, nervous, connective and blood.\n- Plant tissues include epidermis, parenchyma, collenchyma, sclerenchyma, xylem and phloem.\n- Amoeba, Euglena and Paramecium are unicellular.\n- Volvox is a colonial organism and Spirogyra is filamentous.\n- Division of labour increases efficiency in multicellular organisms.\n- Prokaryotic cells lack a nuclear membrane and membrane bound organelles.',
