  {
    'subject': 'Biology',
    'chapterTitle': 'Cell Division: Mitosis and Meiosis',
    'body': 'Cell division is the process by which a parent cell divides to produce daughter cells. It is the basis of growth, repair of damaged tissues, asexual reproduction and the formation of gametes. Before any division, the cell passes through interphase, a period of intense activity often wrongly described as a resting stage. Interphase has three parts: G1, in which the cell grows and makes proteins and organelles; S phase, in which DNA replicates so that each chromosome becomes two identical chromatids joined at a centromere; and G2, in which the cell prepares the spindle proteins and checks the copied DNA.\n\nMitosis is division of the nucleus that produces two daughter nuclei each with exactly the same number and kind of chromosomes as the parent nucleus. It occurs in somatic or body cells, in the meristems of plants at root and shoot tips, and in the bone marrow and skin of animals. The stages are prophase, metaphase, anaphase and telophase, usually remembered as PMAT. In prophase the chromosomes shorten and thicken and become visible as two chromatids, the nucleolus disappears, the nuclear membrane breaks down and the spindle begins to form. In metaphase the chromosomes line up singly along the equator of the spindle and attach by their centromeres to the spindle fibres. In anaphase the centromeres divide and the sister chromatids are pulled apart to opposite poles by the shortening spindle fibres. In telophase the chromatids reach the poles, lengthen, a nuclear membrane reforms around each group, the nucleolus reappears and the spindle disappears. Cytokinesis then divides the cytoplasm; in animal cells the membrane constricts by furrowing, while in plant cells a cell plate forms from the centre outwards.\n\nMeiosis is a reduction division in which one diploid cell produces four haploid cells. It occurs in the reproductive organs, that is the anthers and ovules of flowering plants and the testes and ovaries of animals, and it is essential for the production of gametes. Meiosis has two successive divisions. Meiosis I is the reduction division: in prophase I homologous chromosomes pair to form bivalents in a process called synapsis, and crossing over occurs at the chiasmata so that genetic material is exchanged; in metaphase I the bivalents line up in pairs at the equator; in anaphase I whole homologous chromosomes, not chromatids, move to opposite poles, so the chromosome number is halved; telophase I completes the first division. Meiosis II resembles mitosis and separates the chromatids, producing four haploid daughter cells that are genetically different from one another. This variation arises from crossing over and from the independent assortment of chromosomes, and it is the raw material of evolution.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES AND EXAM STYLE QUESTIONS\n\nExample 1: A cell with 24 chromosomes undergoes mitosis. How many chromosomes will each daughter cell contain, and how many will a gamete of the same organism contain?\nAnswer: Each daughter cell of mitosis will contain 24 chromosomes because mitosis is equational. A gamete formed by meiosis will contain 12 chromosomes because meiosis halves the diploid number.\n\nExample 2: State three differences between mitosis and meiosis.\nAnswer: (i) Mitosis gives two daughter cells while meiosis gives four. (ii) Mitosis maintains the chromosome number while meiosis halves it. (iii) Daughter cells of mitosis are genetically identical to the parent while those of meiosis are genetically different. (iv) Mitosis occurs in somatic cells; meiosis occurs in reproductive cells.\n\nExample 3: Toluwani observed a stained root tip of onion and saw chromosomes arranged singly along the equator. Name the stage and give a reason.\nAnswer: The stage is metaphase of mitosis, because the chromosomes are arranged singly, not in homologous pairs, along the equatorial plate.\n\nExample 4: Explain why crossing over is biologically important.\nAnswer: Crossing over exchanges segments between non sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes, producing new combinations of genes. This creates genetic variation, which increases the chances of survival of a species under changing conditions and provides material on which natural selection acts.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- Cell division allows growth, repair, asexual reproduction and gamete formation.\n- Interphase includes G1, S and G2 phases and is not a resting stage.\n- DNA replicates during the S phase.\n- A chromosome after replication has two chromatids joined at a centromere.\n- Mitosis produces two genetically identical diploid daughter cells.\n- The stages of mitosis are prophase, metaphase, anaphase and telophase.\n- In prophase the nuclear membrane and nucleolus disappear.\n- In metaphase chromosomes align singly at the equator.\n- In anaphase centromeres divide and chromatids move to the poles.\n- In telophase nuclear membranes reform around the two groups.\n- Cytokinesis in animal cells is by cleavage furrow.\n- Cytokinesis in plant cells is by formation of a cell plate.\n- Mitosis occurs in meristems, bone marrow and skin.\n- Meiosis is a reduction division producing four haploid cells.\n- Meiosis occurs in anthers, ovules, testes and ovaries.\n- Meiosis has two divisions, meiosis I and meiosis II.\n- Homologous chromosomes pair during prophase I in synapsis.\n- A pair of homologous chromosomes is called a bivalent.\n- Crossing over occurs at points called chiasmata.\n- Independent assortment also increases variation.\n- Anaphase I separates whole chromosomes; anaphase II separates chromatids.\n- Haploid means one set of chromosomes, written n.\n- Diploid means two sets of chromosomes, written 2n.\n- Fertilisation restores the diploid number.\n- Human diploid number is 46 and haploid number is 23.\n- Non disjunction is the failure of chromosomes to separate properly.\n- Down syndrome results from non disjunction of chromosome 21.\n- Meiosis provides genetic variation essential for evolution.\n- Onion root tip and locust testis are common laboratory materials for studying division.\n- Acetic orcein or acetocarmine is used to stain chromosomes.',
    'source': 'AI-generated study notes',
    'license': 'Internal use',
