  {
    'subject': 'Biology',
    'chapterTitle': 'Nutrition in Plants: Photosynthesis and Mineral Nutrition',
    'body': 'Nutrition is the process by which organisms obtain and use food for energy, growth and repair. Green plants are autotrophic, meaning they manufacture their own organic food from simple inorganic raw materials. The most important form of autotrophic nutrition is photosynthesis, in which carbon dioxide and water are converted into glucose and oxygen using light energy trapped by chlorophyll. The balanced equation is 6CO2 + 6H2O, in the presence of light and chlorophyll, giving C6H12O6 + 6O2. The reaction takes place in the chloroplasts, mainly in the palisade mesophyll cells of the leaf.\n\nThe leaf is beautifully adapted for photosynthesis. It is broad and flat to provide a large surface area for absorbing light and carbon dioxide; it is thin so gases diffuse quickly to all cells; it has a transparent cuticle and epidermis that allow light to pass through; the palisade cells are elongated, packed closely near the upper surface and crowded with chloroplasts; the spongy mesophyll has large air spaces for gaseous diffusion; numerous stomata, mostly on the lower surface, allow exchange of gases; and a network of veins containing xylem and phloem supplies water and mineral salts and removes manufactured food.\n\nPhotosynthesis occurs in two stages. The light dependent stage takes place in the grana of the chloroplast, where chlorophyll absorbs light and uses it to split water into hydrogen and oxygen in a process called photolysis; oxygen is released as a by product and ATP and reduced NADP are formed. The light independent stage, or Calvin cycle, occurs in the stroma, where carbon dioxide is fixed and reduced using the hydrogen and ATP from the first stage to form glucose. The glucose formed is quickly converted to starch for storage, to sucrose for transport, to cellulose for cell walls, or is used in respiration.\n\nFour factors limit the rate of photosynthesis: light intensity, carbon dioxide concentration, temperature and chlorophyll content, and water supply. A limiting factor is the one in shortest supply that holds back the rate. Beyond an optimum temperature of about 35 degrees Celsius the enzymes involved are denatured and the rate falls sharply.\n\nPlants also need mineral salts, absorbed from the soil solution by root hairs mainly through active transport. Nitrogen is needed for proteins and chlorophyll; its deficiency causes stunted growth and yellowing of older leaves, known as chlorosis. Phosphorus is needed for ATP, DNA and root growth; deficiency gives poor root systems and purplish leaves. Potassium is needed for enzyme action and stomatal control; deficiency causes mottled and scorched leaf margins. Magnesium and iron are needed for chlorophyll formation; deficiency causes chlorosis of young leaves. Calcium strengthens cell walls, and sulphur is required for certain proteins. Insectivorous plants such as Utricularia, Drosera and Nepenthes grow in nitrogen poor swampy soils and trap insects to supplement their nitrogen supply, although they still photosynthesise.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES AND EXAM STYLE QUESTIONS\n\nExample 1: Describe how you would show that chlorophyll is necessary for photosynthesis.\nAnswer: Take a potted variegated plant such as Coleus and destarch it by keeping it in darkness for 48 hours. Expose it to sunlight for about six hours. Detach a leaf, draw its pattern showing green and non green areas, kill it in boiling water, decolourise it in hot alcohol using a water bath, soften it in warm water, spread it on a white tile and add iodine solution. Only the parts that were green turn blue black, showing that starch, and hence photosynthesis, occurred only where chlorophyll was present.\n\nExample 2: In an experiment Oyedele counted bubbles of gas released by a water plant at different distances from a lamp and obtained more bubbles as the lamp was brought nearer. Explain the observation and name the gas.\nAnswer: The gas is oxygen. As the lamp is brought nearer, the light intensity increases, so more light energy is trapped by chlorophyll and the rate of photosynthesis rises, releasing more oxygen per minute. Eventually the rate levels off when another factor, such as carbon dioxide concentration, becomes limiting.\n\nExample 3: A farmer near Zetra plantation notices that older leaves of his maize are turning yellow while the plants remain stunted. Which mineral is deficient and how can it be corrected?\nAnswer: Nitrogen is deficient. It can be corrected by applying nitrogenous fertiliser such as urea or ammonium sulphate, by adding organic manure, or by rotating with a leguminous crop that fixes nitrogen.\n\nExample 4: State three differences between photosynthesis and respiration.\nAnswer: Photosynthesis builds up food while respiration breaks it down; photosynthesis takes in carbon dioxide and releases oxygen while respiration does the reverse; photosynthesis stores energy while respiration releases energy; photosynthesis occurs only in green cells in light while respiration occurs in all living cells at all times.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- Autotrophic nutrition means making food from inorganic materials.\n- Photosynthesis is the manufacture of food using light energy and chlorophyll.\n- Equation: 6CO2 + 6H2O gives C6H12O6 + 6O2 in light and chlorophyll.\n- Raw materials are carbon dioxide and water.\n- Products are glucose and oxygen.\n- Photosynthesis occurs in chloroplasts, mainly in palisade cells.\n- The light stage occurs in the grana and the dark stage in the stroma.\n- Photolysis is the splitting of water by light energy.\n- ATP and reduced NADP are made in the light stage.\n- The Calvin cycle fixes carbon dioxide into glucose.\n- Limiting factors are light, carbon dioxide, temperature and water.\n- The leaf is broad and thin for maximum light absorption and quick diffusion.\n- Stomata are mostly on the lower epidermis of the leaf.\n- Guard cells control the opening and closing of stomata.\n- Glucose is stored as starch and transported as sucrose.\n- Iodine solution turns blue black with starch.\n- Alcohol removes chlorophyll before the starch test.\n- Destarching is done by keeping the plant in darkness.\n- Nitrogen deficiency causes chlorosis and stunted growth.\n- Phosphorus is needed for ATP, DNA and root development.\n- Potassium controls enzyme action and stomatal movement.\n- Magnesium and iron are needed for chlorophyll formation.\n- Calcium strengthens cell walls and middle lamella.\n- Minerals are absorbed by root hairs mainly by active transport.\n- Root hairs give a large surface area for absorption.\n- Chemosynthetic bacteria such as Nitrosomonas make food using chemical energy.\n- Insectivorous plants trap insects for nitrogen in poor soils.\n- Examples of insectivorous plants are Nepenthes, Drosera and Utricularia.\n- Photosynthesis is the ultimate source of all food and fossil fuel energy.\n- Photosynthesis maintains the balance of oxygen and carbon dioxide in nature.',
    'source': 'AI-generated study notes',
    'license': 'Internal use',
