  {
    'subject': 'Biology',
    'chapterTitle': 'Nutrition in Animals: Feeding Types and the Human Digestive System',
    'body': 'Animals are heterotrophic; they cannot manufacture their own food and must obtain ready made organic matter. Heterotrophic nutrition takes several forms. Holozoic nutrition involves taking in solid food and digesting it internally, and it is seen in Amoeba, insects and all vertebrates. Saprophytic nutrition involves feeding on dead organic matter by secreting enzymes onto it and absorbing the soluble products, as in Rhizopus and Mucor. Parasitic nutrition involves living on or inside another organism, the host, and obtaining nourishment from it, as in tapeworm, Plasmodium, Ascaris and mistletoe. Symbiotic nutrition is a partnership of mutual benefit, as in lichens, root nodule bacteria and the cellulose digesting bacteria in the rumen of cattle.\n\nAmong holozoic feeders the mouth parts and teeth are adapted to diet. Herbivores such as goats and cows have sharp incisors and a horny pad for cropping grass, a diastema, and broad ridged premolars and molars for grinding; their jaws move sideways. Carnivores such as dogs and lions have pointed canines for piercing and holding prey and carnassial teeth for shearing flesh; their jaws move up and down. Omnivores such as humans have all four types of teeth. Dentition in humans is described by the dental formula; for an adult it is i2/2 c1/1 pm2/2 m3/3 giving a total of 32 teeth, while a child has 20 milk teeth with the formula i2/2 c1/1 m2/2.\n\nA balanced diet contains carbohydrates for energy, proteins for growth and repair, fats and oils for energy and insulation, vitamins for protection against deficiency diseases, mineral salts for various body functions, water as a solvent and medium for reactions, and roughage to add bulk and prevent constipation. Deficiency of protein causes kwashiorkor and marasmus, lack of vitamin A causes night blindness, vitamin C deficiency causes scurvy, vitamin D deficiency causes rickets, and lack of iron causes anaemia while lack of iodine causes goitre.\n\nThe human alimentary canal begins at the mouth, where teeth chew food and saliva containing the enzyme ptyalin, or salivary amylase, converts cooked starch to maltose in a slightly alkaline medium. The bolus passes down the oesophagus by peristalsis. In the stomach gastric juice contains hydrochloric acid, which kills bacteria and provides an acidic medium of about pH 2, pepsin which converts proteins to peptides, and rennin which curdles milk in infants. The chyme then enters the duodenum, where bile from the liver, stored in the gall bladder, neutralises the acid and emulsifies fats, while pancreatic juice supplies amylase, trypsin and lipase. In the ileum, intestinal juice completes digestion with maltase, sucrase, lactase, erepsin and lipase, giving glucose, amino acids, fatty acids and glycerol.\n\nAbsorption occurs mainly in the ileum, which is long and coiled and lined with millions of villi. Each villus has a thin epithelium one cell thick, a rich network of blood capillaries for glucose and amino acids, and a lacteal for fatty acids and glycerol. Water and mineral salts are absorbed in the colon, and undigested matter is egested as faeces through the rectum and anus. Assimilation is the use of absorbed food: glucose is respired or stored as glycogen in the liver and muscles, amino acids build new protoplasm and enzymes, and excess is deaminated in the liver to urea.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES AND EXAM STYLE QUESTIONS\n\nExample 1: Name the enzyme, substrate, product and medium of action for gastric protein digestion.\nAnswer: Enzyme pepsin, substrate protein, product peptides, medium acidic, supplied by hydrochloric acid in gastric juice.\n\nExample 2: Oyedele complained of pain after his gall bladder was removed. Explain how the removal would affect the digestion of a plate of fried yam.\nAnswer: Bile would no longer be stored and released in concentrated amounts, so fats in the fried yam would not be efficiently emulsified into small droplets. Lipase would then act slowly because of the reduced surface area, and fat digestion and absorption would be poor, leading to fatty stools.\n\nExample 3: List four adaptations of the ileum for absorption.\nAnswer: It is very long and coiled to increase surface area; it has numerous villi and microvilli; the epithelium is one cell thick for rapid diffusion; each villus has a dense capillary network and a lacteal to maintain a steep concentration gradient.\n\nExample 4: A worker at Zetra food factory eats mainly cassava with little fish or beans. Name the likely deficiency disease and state two symptoms.\nAnswer: Kwashiorkor, caused by protein deficiency. Symptoms include oedema of the limbs and face, a swollen belly, thin reddish hair, retarded growth and loss of appetite.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- Heterotrophic nutrition means feeding on ready made organic food.\n- Types are holozoic, saprophytic, parasitic and symbiotic.\n- Holozoic feeding includes ingestion, digestion, absorption, assimilation and egestion.\n- Saprophytes feed on dead organic matter by extracellular digestion.\n- Parasites live on or in a host and cause it harm.\n- Symbionts live together for mutual benefit.\n- Herbivores have a diastema and ridged molars.\n- Carnivores have long canines and carnassial teeth.\n- Adult human dental formula is i2/2 c1/1 pm2/2 m3/3 equals 32 teeth.\n- Milk teeth number 20 and lack premolars.\n- A balanced diet supplies all food classes in correct proportions.\n- Carbohydrates and fats are energy giving foods.\n- Proteins are body building foods.\n- Vitamins and minerals are protective foods.\n- Roughage prevents constipation.\n- Ptyalin digests cooked starch to maltose in the mouth.\n- Peristalsis moves food along the gut by muscular waves.\n- Hydrochloric acid kills bacteria and provides an acid medium.\n- Pepsin digests protein to peptides in the stomach.\n- Rennin curdles milk in young mammals.\n- Bile is made in the liver and stored in the gall bladder.\n- Bile contains no enzymes but emulsifies fats.\n- Pancreatic juice contains amylase, trypsin and lipase.\n- Final products of digestion are glucose, amino acids, fatty acids and glycerol.\n- Absorption occurs mainly in the ileum through villi.\n- Fatty acids and glycerol enter the lacteals.\n- Glucose and amino acids enter the blood capillaries.\n- Water and salts are absorbed in the colon.\n- Excess amino acids are deaminated in the liver to form urea.\n- Glycogen is the storage carbohydrate of the liver and muscles.',
    'source': 'AI-generated study notes',
    'license': 'Internal use',
  },

  {
    'subject': 'Biology',
    'chapterTitle': 'Transport Systems in Plants',
    'body': 'All living organisms must move materials from where they are absorbed or made to where they are used or stored. In small organisms such as Amoeba, simple diffusion over the body surface is enough, but in large multicellular plants a specialised vascular system is required. The vascular tissues of flowering plants are the xylem and the phloem, which together form the vascular bundles.\n\nXylem conducts water and dissolved mineral salts from the roots upward to the leaves, and it also gives mechanical support. It is made of vessels and tracheids, which are dead, hollow, lignified tubes without cross walls in the case of vessels, plus xylem fibres and xylem parenchyma. Phloem conducts manufactured food, mainly sucrose and amino acids, from the leaves to all other parts in a process called translocation, and this movement is in both directions. Phloem consists of living sieve tubes with perforated sieve plates, companion cells rich in mitochondria, phloem fibres and phloem parenchyma. In a dicotyledonous stem the bundles are arranged in a ring with xylem inside and phloem outside, separated by cambium, while in a monocotyledonous stem the bundles are scattered and have no cambium. In the root, xylem forms a central star shape with phloem between the arms.\n\nWater enters through root hairs by osmosis because the cell sap of the root hair is more concentrated than the soil solution. It then passes from cell to cell across the cortex to the xylem, and moves up the stem in a continuous column. Three forces drive this ascent of sap: root pressure, which pushes water up from below; capillarity in the narrow xylem vessels; and above all transpiration pull, the suction created as water evaporates from the leaves, aided by the cohesion of water molecules to one another and their adhesion to the walls of the xylem.\n\nTranspiration is the loss of water vapour from the aerial parts of a plant. About 90 per cent occurs through the stomata, with small amounts through the cuticle and through lenticels in woody stems. It is increased by high temperature, low humidity, moving air, bright light which opens stomata, and a large leaf surface area. It is reduced by high humidity, still air, darkness and wilting. Transpiration is useful because it cools the plant, creates the pull that lifts water and mineral salts, and helps maintain turgidity, but excessive transpiration causes wilting and death. Xerophytes such as cactus reduce water loss by having thick cuticles, sunken stomata, reduced or spiny leaves, and succulent water storing stems. Guttation is the loss of liquid water through hydathodes at leaf margins, usually at night when transpiration is low and root pressure is high.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES AND EXAM STYLE QUESTIONS\n\nExample 1: State four differences between xylem and phloem.\nAnswer: Xylem conducts water and mineral salts while phloem conducts manufactured food; xylem cells are dead and lignified while phloem sieve tubes are living; xylem transport is unidirectional and upward while phloem transport is bidirectional; xylem provides mechanical support while phloem does not.\n\nExample 2: Toluwani set up an experiment with a leafy shoot in a potometer and observed that the bubble moved faster when a fan was directed at the shoot. Explain.\nAnswer: Moving air removes the saturated layer of water vapour around the leaves, steepening the diffusion gradient between the air spaces and the atmosphere, so transpiration increases. Faster water loss produces a stronger transpiration pull, so water is absorbed more rapidly and the bubble moves faster.\n\nExample 3: A ring of bark was removed from a tree at the Zetra estate. After some weeks a swelling appeared just above the ring. Explain.\nAnswer: Removing the bark removed the phloem, which lies outside the cambium. Manufactured food moving down from the leaves could not pass the ring, so it accumulated above it, causing the swelling. The xylem was untouched, so water transport continued for a while.\n\nExample 4: Why does a wilted plant recover when watered in the evening?\nAnswer: Watering restores the soil water potential, so water enters the root hairs by osmosis, the cells regain turgor pressure, and the leaves and young stems become firm again. Recovery is faster in the evening because transpiration is low.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- Transport moves materials from absorption or production sites to use or storage sites.\n- Vascular tissues of plants are xylem and phloem.\n- Xylem carries water and mineral salts upward.\n- Xylem vessels are dead, hollow and lignified.\n- Lignin gives strength and mechanical support.\n- Phloem carries manufactured food in both directions.\n- Sieve tubes are living and have sieve plates.\n- Companion cells supply energy for translocation.\n- Cambium lies between xylem and phloem in dicots.\n- Dicot stems have bundles in a ring; monocot stems have scattered bundles.\n- Root hairs increase the surface area for water absorption.\n- Water enters the root hair by osmosis.\n- Mineral salts enter mainly by active transport.\n- Ascent of sap is due to root pressure, capillarity and transpiration pull.\n- Cohesion and adhesion keep the water column unbroken.\n- Transpiration is loss of water vapour from aerial parts.\n- Most transpiration occurs through stomata.\n- Lenticels allow gaseous exchange and slight water loss in woody stems.\n- Transpiration rate rises with heat, wind, light and low humidity.\n- A potometer measures the rate of water uptake.\n- Guttation is the loss of liquid water through hydathodes.\n- Transpiration cools the plant and aids mineral transport.\n- Excessive transpiration causes wilting.\n- Xerophytes have sunken stomata and thick cuticles.\n- Hydrophytes have many stomata on the upper surface.\n- Turgidity supports herbaceous plants.\n- Ringing experiments demonstrate the role of phloem.\n- Dye experiments with eosin demonstrate the pathway of xylem.\n- Translocation requires energy from respiration.\n- Wilting occurs when water loss exceeds water absorption.',
    'source': 'AI-generated study notes',
    'license': 'Internal use',
  },

  {
    'subject': 'Biology',
    'chapterTitle': 'Transport Systems in Animals: Blood, Circulation and the Heart',
    'body': 'In animals larger and more active than a flatworm, diffusion alone cannot supply cells with oxygen and food quickly enough, so a circulatory system develops. A circulatory system has three parts: a transport medium, which is blood; a system of tubes, the blood vessels; and a pump, the heart. In insects the system is open, with blood bathing the organs directly in a haemocoel, while in vertebrates it is closed, with blood confined to vessels. In fish the circulation is single: blood passes through the heart once per complete circuit. In mammals, birds and adult amphibians the circulation is double: the pulmonary circuit carries blood from the heart to the lungs and back, and the systemic circuit carries it from the heart to the rest of the body and back. Double circulation maintains high pressure and allows a high metabolic rate.\n\nHuman blood consists of plasma and cells. Plasma is about 90 per cent water and carries dissolved food, hormones, antibodies, carbon dioxide as hydrogen carbonate, urea, plasma proteins such as fibrinogen and albumin, and heat. Red blood cells, or erythrocytes, are biconcave discs without nuclei in mammals, packed with the red pigment haemoglobin; they are made in the red bone marrow, live about 120 days and are destroyed in the liver and spleen. Their shape gives a large surface area and their lack of a nucleus creates space for more haemoglobin. Haemoglobin combines reversibly with oxygen to form oxyhaemoglobin in the lungs and releases it in the tissues. White blood cells, or leucocytes, are nucleated and defend the body: phagocytes engulf and digest bacteria, while lymphocytes produce antibodies. Platelets are cell fragments that help in clotting: when a vessel is cut, platelets and damaged tissue release thromboplastin, which with calcium ions and vitamin K converts prothrombin to thrombin, and thrombin converts soluble fibrinogen to insoluble fibrin, which traps blood cells to form a clot.\n\nThere are three kinds of blood vessels. Arteries carry blood away from the heart, have thick muscular elastic walls, narrow lumens, no valves except at the base of the aorta and pulmonary artery, and carry blood under high pressure in spurts. Veins carry blood to the heart, have thinner walls, wider lumens, semilunar valves to prevent backflow, and carry blood under low pressure in a steady flow. Capillaries are one cell thick and permit exchange of materials between blood and tissue fluid.\n\nThe mammalian heart is a four chambered muscular organ enclosed in a pericardium. Deoxygenated blood from the body enters the right atrium through the venae cavae, passes through the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle, and is pumped through the pulmonary artery to the lungs. Oxygenated blood returns by the pulmonary veins to the left atrium, passes the bicuspid or mitral valve into the left ventricle, and is pumped through the aorta to the whole body. The left ventricle has the thickest wall because it pumps blood the greatest distance. The cardiac cycle consists of atrial systole, ventricular systole and diastole, and the heart beat originates in the sino atrial node, the natural pacemaker. Human ABO blood groups are A, B, AB and O; group O is the universal donor and AB the universal recipient, and the rhesus factor must also be matched during transfusion.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES AND EXAM STYLE QUESTIONS\n\nExample 1: Explain why the wall of the left ventricle is thicker than that of the right ventricle.\nAnswer: The left ventricle pumps oxygenated blood through the aorta to the whole body, which requires a high pressure to overcome the resistance of a long circuit, so its muscular wall is thick. The right ventricle pumps only to the nearby lungs at lower pressure, so its wall is thinner.\n\nExample 2: Toluwani cut his finger and the bleeding stopped after a few minutes. Outline the process involved.\nAnswer: Platelets adhere to the damaged area and, with the injured tissue, release thromboplastin. In the presence of calcium ions and vitamin K, thromboplastin converts prothrombin to thrombin. Thrombin converts soluble fibrinogen into insoluble fibrin threads, which form a mesh that traps red blood cells and seals the wound as a clot.\n\nExample 3: A patient of blood group A is to receive blood. From which groups can he safely receive and why?\nAnswer: From groups A and O. His plasma contains anti B antibodies, so blood carrying antigen B, that is groups B and AB, would be agglutinated. Group O red cells carry no A or B antigens, so they are safe.\n\nExample 4: Why do the red blood cells of mammals lack a nucleus?\nAnswer: The absence of a nucleus creates more space for haemoglobin, increasing oxygen carrying capacity, and allows the cell to keep its flexible biconcave shape so it can squeeze through narrow capillaries.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- A circulatory system consists of a medium, vessels and a pump.\n- Insects have an open circulatory system with a haemocoel.\n- Vertebrates have a closed circulatory system.\n- Fish have single circulation; mammals have double circulation.\n- Double circulation separates oxygenated and deoxygenated blood.\n- Blood is made of plasma, red cells, white cells and platelets.\n- Plasma is about 90 per cent water.\n- Red blood cells are biconcave and lack nuclei in mammals.\n- Haemoglobin is an iron containing respiratory pigment.\n- Oxyhaemoglobin forms in the lungs and dissociates in the tissues.\n- Red cells are made in red bone marrow and destroyed in liver and spleen.\n- White cells include phagocytes and lymphocytes.\n- Phagocytes engulf bacteria; lymphocytes produce antibodies.\n- Platelets initiate blood clotting.\n- Clotting sequence: thromboplastin, prothrombin to thrombin, fibrinogen to fibrin.\n- Vitamin K and calcium ions are essential for clotting.\n- Haemophilia is a hereditary failure of blood to clot.\n- Arteries carry blood away from the heart at high pressure.\n- Veins carry blood to the heart and contain valves.\n- Capillaries are one cell thick for exchange of materials.\n- The pulmonary artery is the only artery carrying deoxygenated blood.\n- The pulmonary vein is the only vein carrying oxygenated blood.\n- The heart has four chambers: two atria and two ventricles.\n- The tricuspid valve lies between right atrium and right ventricle.\n- The bicuspid or mitral valve lies on the left side.\n- Semilunar valves prevent backflow into the ventricles.\n- The sino atrial node is the pacemaker of the heart.\n- Systole is contraction and diastole is relaxation.\n- Blood groups are A, B, AB and O.\n- Group O is the universal donor and AB the universal recipient.\n- Lymph is tissue fluid that drains into lymph vessels and returns to the blood.',
    'source': 'AI-generated study notes',
    'license': 'Internal use',
  },

  {
    'subject': 'Biology',
    'chapterTitle': 'Respiration: Aerobic, Anaerobic and Gaseous Exchange',
    'body': 'Respiration is the breakdown of food substances within living cells to release energy. It must be clearly separated from breathing, which is only the mechanical movement of air in and out of the body, and from gaseous exchange, which is the diffusion of oxygen and carbon dioxide across a surface. The energy released is stored in the form of adenosine triphosphate, ATP, which is used for muscular contraction, active transport, growth, cell division, nerve impulse transmission and the maintenance of body temperature in warm blooded animals.\n\nAerobic respiration requires oxygen and completely oxidises glucose to carbon dioxide and water, releasing about 2900 kilojoules of energy per mole, or up to 38 molecules of ATP. The equation is C6H12O6 + 6O2 giving 6CO2 + 6H2O + energy. It occurs in two stages. Glycolysis takes place in the cytoplasm and does not require oxygen; one glucose molecule is split into two molecules of pyruvic acid, yielding a small amount of ATP. The Krebs cycle and the electron transport chain occur in the mitochondria, where pyruvic acid is completely oxidised and most of the ATP is produced.\n\nAnaerobic respiration occurs in the absence of oxygen and releases far less energy because the food is only partly broken down. In yeast and other micro organisms, glucose is converted to ethanol and carbon dioxide, releasing about 118 kilojoules per mole; this is alcoholic fermentation and is the basis of brewing, wine making and bread making. In animal muscle during vigorous exercise, glucose is converted to lactic acid, which accumulates and causes muscle fatigue and cramp. The extra oxygen needed afterwards to oxidise the lactic acid is called the oxygen debt, and this is why panting continues after a race.\n\nGaseous exchange surfaces share common features: they are thin, moist, permeable, have a large surface area, and are well supplied with a transport medium to maintain a diffusion gradient. In plants, exchange occurs through stomata in leaves, lenticels in woody stems and the general surface of young roots; there is no special transport system for gases because the air spaces in the spongy mesophyll connect directly to every cell. In Amoeba and other protozoa, exchange is by simple diffusion across the plasma membrane. Earthworms use the moist skin, insects use a system of tracheae opening at spiracles, fish use gills with numerous gill filaments and lamellae supplied by capillaries, amphibians use skin, lungs and buccal cavity, and mammals use lungs.\n\nIn the mammal, air enters through the nostrils, passes the pharynx, larynx, trachea, bronchi and bronchioles, and reaches the alveoli, which are tiny air sacs surrounded by capillaries. Inspiration occurs when the external intercostal muscles contract, raising the ribs upward and outward while the diaphragm muscles contract and flatten; the thoracic volume increases, pressure falls and air rushes in. Expiration is largely passive; the muscles relax, the ribs fall, the diaphragm domes upward, volume decreases, pressure rises and air is forced out. Inhaled air contains about 21 per cent oxygen and 0.03 per cent carbon dioxide, while exhaled air contains about 16 per cent oxygen, 4 per cent carbon dioxide and is warm and saturated with water vapour.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES AND EXAM STYLE QUESTIONS\n\nExample 1: State three differences between aerobic and anaerobic respiration.\nAnswer: Aerobic respiration requires oxygen while anaerobic does not; aerobic gives carbon dioxide and water while anaerobic gives ethanol and carbon dioxide in yeast or lactic acid in muscle; aerobic releases much more energy, about 2900 kilojoules, compared with about 118 kilojoules in anaerobic respiration.\n\nExample 2: After running a 400 metre race Oyedele continued to breathe heavily for several minutes. Explain.\nAnswer: During the race his muscles respired anaerobically because oxygen supply was insufficient, producing lactic acid. He therefore built up an oxygen debt. Continued heavy breathing supplies the extra oxygen needed to oxidise the accumulated lactic acid to carbon dioxide and water and to restore normal muscle function.\n\nExample 3: Describe an experiment to show that carbon dioxide is produced during respiration in germinating seeds.\nAnswer: Place germinating seeds in a conical flask connected by tubing to a test tube of lime water; place boiled dead seeds in a similar control set up. Draw air first through soda lime to remove atmospheric carbon dioxide. After some hours the lime water connected to the living seeds turns milky, while the control remains clear, showing that respiring seeds release carbon dioxide.\n\nExample 4: Give four features that make the alveolus efficient for gaseous exchange.\nAnswer: The alveoli are numerous, giving a very large total surface area; their walls are one cell thick for rapid diffusion; they are moist so gases dissolve before diffusing; and they are richly supplied with blood capillaries which continually remove oxygen and bring carbon dioxide, maintaining a steep diffusion gradient.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- Respiration is the release of energy from food in living cells.\n- Breathing is a mechanical process; respiration is a chemical one.\n- ATP is the energy currency of the cell.\n- Aerobic respiration requires oxygen and gives carbon dioxide and water.\n- Aerobic respiration yields about 2900 kilojoules per mole of glucose.\n- Glycolysis occurs in the cytoplasm and does not need oxygen.\n- The Krebs cycle occurs in the mitochondrial matrix.\n- The electron transport chain occurs on the cristae.\n- Anaerobic respiration in yeast gives ethanol and carbon dioxide.\n- Anaerobic respiration in muscle gives lactic acid.\n- Oxygen debt is repaid by continued deep breathing after exercise.\n- Fermentation is used in brewing and bread making.\n- Respiratory surfaces are thin, moist, permeable and large.\n- Plants exchange gases through stomata, lenticels and root surfaces.\n- Amoeba exchanges gases by diffusion through the cell membrane.\n- Earthworms respire through moist skin.\n- Insects use tracheae, tracheoles and spiracles.\n- Fish use gills with filaments and lamellae.\n- Counter current flow makes fish gills highly efficient.\n- Mammals use lungs containing millions of alveoli.\n- The trachea is supported by rings of cartilage.\n- The epiglottis prevents food from entering the trachea.\n- Inspiration involves contraction of the diaphragm and external intercostals.\n- Expiration is mainly passive relaxation.\n- Inhaled air has about 21 per cent oxygen; exhaled about 16 per cent.\n- Exhaled air is warm and saturated with water vapour.\n- Lime water turns milky in the presence of carbon dioxide.\n- Anhydrous cobalt chloride paper turns from blue to pink with water.\n- Respiratory quotient compares carbon dioxide released with oxygen used.\n- Smoking damages the alveoli and causes emphysema and lung cancer.',
    'source': 'AI-generated study notes',
    'license': 'Internal use',
  },

  {
    'subject': 'Biology',
    'chapterTitle': 'Excretion in Plants and Animals',
    'body': 'Excretion is the removal from the body of the waste products of metabolism, together with excess substances that would become harmful if allowed to accumulate. It is not the same as egestion, which is the removal of undigested food that never entered the cells, nor is it secretion, which is the release of useful substances such as enzymes and hormones. The chief metabolic wastes of animals are carbon dioxide and water from respiration, and nitrogenous wastes such as ammonia, urea and uric acid from the breakdown of excess amino acids. Excretion is closely linked with osmoregulation, the control of the water and salt content of body fluids.\n\nIn animals, excretory organs vary with complexity. Amoeba uses the contractile vacuole for water and the general body surface for other wastes. Flatworms use flame cells, earthworms use nephridia, and insects use Malpighian tubules that empty uric acid into the gut, which is an adaptation to conserve water. In mammals the main excretory organs are the kidneys, the lungs, the skin and the liver. The lungs excrete carbon dioxide and water vapour. The skin excretes sweat containing water, salts and a little urea, and also helps regulate temperature. The liver deaminates excess amino acids to form urea, breaks down worn out red blood cells to form bile pigments, and detoxifies harmful substances such as alcohol and drugs.\n\nThe kidney is the principal organ of excretion and osmoregulation. Each kidney is made of a cortex, a medulla and a pelvis leading to the ureter, and contains about a million nephrons. A nephron consists of a Bowman capsule enclosing a knot of capillaries called the glomerulus, followed by the proximal convoluted tubule, the loop of Henle, the distal convoluted tubule and the collecting duct. Urine formation involves two main steps. First is ultrafiltration: blood arriving through the wide afferent arteriole is under high pressure, and small molecules such as water, glucose, amino acids, salts and urea are forced out into the Bowman capsule while blood cells and large proteins remain behind. Second is selective reabsorption: as the filtrate moves along the tubule, all the glucose, most of the water and the needed salts are reabsorbed into the surrounding capillaries, largely by active transport in the proximal tubule. The remaining fluid, urine, contains urea, excess salts and water, and passes to the bladder for storage before being released through the urethra. The hormone ADH from the pituitary controls water reabsorption in the collecting duct; when the body is dehydrated more ADH is released, more water is reabsorbed and a small volume of concentrated urine is produced.\n\nPlants have no special excretory organs because they produce far less waste; much of their nitrogenous material is reused. Excess oxygen from photosynthesis and carbon dioxide from respiration diffuse out through stomata and lenticels, and excess water is lost by transpiration and guttation. Other wastes are stored in harmless forms: tannins, resins, gums and latex accumulate in bark and heartwood, calcium oxalate crystals are deposited in cells, and many waste products are simply stored in leaves and bark which are later shed. Some of these substances are economically valuable, for example latex for rubber, quinine from cinchona bark, and caffeine from coffee.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES AND EXAM STYLE QUESTIONS\n\nExample 1: Distinguish between excretion, egestion and secretion with one example each.\nAnswer: Excretion is removal of metabolic waste, for example urea in urine. Egestion is removal of undigested food from the alimentary canal, for example faeces. Secretion is the release of a useful substance made by cells, for example insulin or saliva.\n\nExample 2: Glucose was detected in the urine of a man tested at the Zetra clinic. State two possible causes.\nAnswer: (i) Diabetes mellitus, in which insufficient insulin raises blood glucose above the renal threshold so that not all the glucose can be reabsorbed. (ii) Damage to the kidney tubules, so that selective reabsorption of glucose fails.\n\nExample 3: Explain why a person produces small amounts of concentrated urine on a hot day.\nAnswer: On a hot day much water is lost as sweat, so the blood becomes more concentrated. The hypothalamus detects this and the pituitary releases more antidiuretic hormone, which makes the collecting ducts more permeable so that more water is reabsorbed into the blood, leaving a small volume of concentrated urine.\n\nExample 4: Why is the afferent arteriole wider than the efferent arteriole?\nAnswer: The difference in diameter creates high hydrostatic pressure in the glomerulus, which is necessary to force water and small solutes out of the blood into the Bowman capsule during ultrafiltration.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- Excretion removes metabolic wastes from the body.\n- Egestion removes undigested food and is not excretion.\n- Secretion releases useful substances such as hormones and enzymes.\n- Main animal excretory products are carbon dioxide, water, urea and uric acid.\n- Ammonia is very toxic and is excreted by aquatic animals.\n- Urea is less toxic and is the main product in mammals.\n- Uric acid is insoluble and conserves water in insects, birds and reptiles.\n- Amoeba uses the contractile vacuole for osmoregulation.\n- Flatworms use flame cells and earthworms use nephridia.\n- Insects use Malpighian tubules.\n- Mammalian excretory organs are kidney, lungs, skin and liver.\n- The liver deaminates excess amino acids to form urea.\n- The liver detoxifies alcohol and drugs.\n- The functional unit of the kidney is the nephron.\n- The glomerulus is a knot of capillaries in the Bowman capsule.\n- Ultrafiltration occurs in the Bowman capsule.\n- Blood cells and plasma proteins are not filtered.\n- Selective reabsorption occurs mainly in the proximal convoluted tubule.\n- All glucose is normally reabsorbed.\n- The loop of Henle concentrates the urine.\n- ADH from the pituitary controls water reabsorption.\n- Lack of ADH causes diabetes insipidus.\n- Lack of insulin causes diabetes mellitus with glucose in urine.\n- Urine passes from kidney to bladder through the ureter.\n- Urine leaves the body through the urethra.\n- The skin excretes water, salts and traces of urea in sweat.\n- Plants have no specialised excretory organs.\n- Plant wastes include tannins, resins, gums, latex and alkaloids.\n- Plants excrete gases through stomata and lenticels.\n- Leaf fall removes stored wastes from perennial plants.',
    'source': 'AI-generated study notes',
    'license': 'Internal use',
  },

  {
    'subject': 'Biology',
    'chapterTitle': 'Support and Movement',
    'body': 'Support is the ability of an organism to hold its body or parts of it in position against the pull of gravity, while movement is a change in position of the whole organism or of its parts. Support is necessary to maintain shape and form, to hold organs in position, to allow maximum exposure of leaves to sunlight in plants, to resist mechanical stresses such as wind, and to provide a firm framework for the attachment of muscles in animals.\n\nIn plants, support comes from several sources. Turgidity of parenchyma cells provides the main support in herbaceous plants; when the cells are full of water they press against their walls and the tissue becomes firm. Collenchyma has cellulose thickening at the corners and supports young growing stems and leaf stalks while remaining flexible. Sclerenchyma consists of dead cells with thick lignified walls and provides strength in mature regions. Xylem vessels and tracheids are lignified and give both conduction and mechanical support, and in trees the accumulated secondary xylem forms wood. Additional support comes from special structures such as prop roots in maize and mangrove, buttress roots in silk cotton trees, stilt roots, tendrils in passion flower, twining stems in yam, and clasping roots in climbing plants.\n\nIn animals, the supporting structure is the skeleton, of which there are three kinds. A hydrostatic skeleton is found in earthworms and sea anemones, where fluid under pressure in the body cavity works with circular and longitudinal muscles to give shape and permit movement. An exoskeleton is found in arthropods, made of chitin; it supports, protects, prevents desiccation, and provides attachment for muscles, but it must be moulted for growth to occur. An endoskeleton is found in vertebrates and is made of bone and cartilage; it grows with the animal, supports the body, protects delicate organs, provides surfaces for muscle attachment, produces blood cells in the red marrow, and stores calcium and phosphorus.\n\nThe human skeleton has about 206 bones divided into the axial skeleton, which comprises the skull, vertebral column, ribs and sternum, and the appendicular skeleton, which comprises the limbs and the pectoral and pelvic girdles. The vertebral column consists of 7 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 fused sacral and 4 fused caudal vertebrae. The atlas is the first cervical vertebra and allows the nodding of the head, while the axis has an odontoid process that allows the head to rotate. Joints are formed where bones meet. Immovable or fixed joints occur between the bones of the cranium; slightly movable joints occur between the vertebrae, cushioned by cartilage discs; freely movable or synovial joints include the ball and socket joint of the hip and shoulder, which permits movement in all directions, and the hinge joint of the elbow and knee, which permits movement in one plane only. Synovial joints contain synovial fluid to lubricate and cartilage to reduce friction.\n\nMovement of limbs depends on muscles working in antagonistic pairs, because a muscle can only pull, never push. When the biceps contracts and the triceps relaxes, the forearm is flexed; when the triceps contracts and the biceps relaxes, the arm is extended. Tendons attach muscle to bone, while ligaments attach bone to bone at joints.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES AND EXAM STYLE QUESTIONS\n\nExample 1: Explain why an arthropod must moult while a mammal need not.\nAnswer: The arthropod exoskeleton is made of hard, non living chitin that cannot grow. To increase in size, the animal must shed the old covering by ecdysis and expand before the new one hardens. A mammal has a living endoskeleton of bone containing cells that multiply and deposit new matrix, so the skeleton grows with the animal.\n\nExample 2: State three differences between a ball and socket joint and a hinge joint, with examples.\nAnswer: A ball and socket joint permits movement in all planes including rotation, while a hinge joint permits movement in one plane only. Ball and socket occurs at the shoulder and hip; hinge occurs at the elbow, knee and finger joints. The ball and socket has a rounded head fitting into a cup shaped cavity, while the hinge has a convex surface fitting into a concave one.\n\nExample 3: Oyedele placed a fresh bone in dilute hydrochloric acid for two days and found it became soft and flexible. Explain.\nAnswer: The acid dissolved the mineral salts, chiefly calcium phosphate and calcium carbonate, which give the bone its hardness and rigidity. What remained was the organic matrix of collagen, which is soft and flexible.\n\nExample 4: Why do herbaceous plants wilt in the afternoon but recover at night?\nAnswer: In the afternoon transpiration exceeds water absorption, the parenchyma cells lose turgor, and the soft tissues can no longer support the plant, so it wilts. At night transpiration is greatly reduced, absorption continues, the cells regain turgidity and the plant becomes firm again.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- Support keeps the body in position against gravity.\n- Turgidity supports herbaceous plants.\n- Collenchyma has cellulose thickening at the corners.\n- Sclerenchyma cells are dead and heavily lignified.\n- Lignin gives rigidity to xylem and sclerenchyma.\n- Prop roots in maize and buttress roots in silk cotton give extra support.\n- Tendrils and twining stems support climbing plants.\n- Three skeleton types are hydrostatic, exoskeleton and endoskeleton.\n- A hydrostatic skeleton uses fluid pressure, as in the earthworm.\n- An exoskeleton is made of chitin in arthropods.\n- Ecdysis is the moulting of the exoskeleton.\n- The endoskeleton of vertebrates is made of bone and cartilage.\n- The human skeleton has about 206 bones.\n- The axial skeleton includes skull, vertebral column, ribs and sternum.\n- The appendicular skeleton includes limbs and girdles.\n- Cervical vertebrae number seven in almost all mammals.\n- The atlas allows nodding of the head.\n- The axis has an odontoid process allowing rotation.\n- The pectoral girdle bears the scapula and clavicle.\n- The pelvic girdle consists of ilium, ischium and pubis.\n- Fixed joints occur in the skull.\n- Synovial joints are freely movable and contain synovial fluid.\n- Ball and socket joints occur at hip and shoulder.\n- Hinge joints occur at elbow and knee.\n- Tendons join muscle to bone.\n- Ligaments join bone to bone.\n- Muscles work in antagonistic pairs.\n- Biceps flexes and triceps extends the forearm.\n- Bone stores calcium and phosphorus and makes blood cells.\n- Cartilage reduces friction at joint surfaces.\n- Functions of the skeleton include support, protection, movement and blood cell formation.',
    'source': 'AI-generated study notes',
    'license': 'Internal use',
  },

