    'subject': 'Biology',
    'chapterTitle': 'Reproduction in Plants',
    'body': 'Reproduction is the process by which living organisms give rise to new individuals of their kind, ensuring the continuity of the species. In plants it occurs in two broad forms: asexual and sexual.\n\nAsexual reproduction involves only one parent and no fusion of gametes, so the offspring are genetically identical to the parent and are called clones. It occurs by fission in unicellular organisms, by budding in yeast, by spore formation in Rhizopus and ferns, by fragmentation in Spirogyra, and above all by vegetative propagation in flowering plants. Natural vegetative propagation uses modified stems such as rhizomes in ginger and canna, stem tubers in Irish potato, bulbs in onion, corms in cocoyam, runners in sweet potato and suckers in banana and pineapple, as well as root tubers in cassava and sweet potato and bulbils in Bryophyllum. Artificial methods include cutting, layering, grafting and budding, which are widely used in the propagation of cassava, sugarcane, citrus, mango and cocoa. The advantages of vegetative propagation are that it is quick, only one parent is needed, desirable characters are preserved exactly, and the young plant is supported by food stored in the parent organ. Its disadvantages are overcrowding, rapid exhaustion of soil nutrients, easy spread of disease and the absence of genetic variation.\n\nSexual reproduction involves the fusion of male and female gametes and produces offspring showing variation. In flowering plants the flower is the reproductive organ. A typical flower has four whorls: the calyx of sepals, which protects the bud; the corolla of petals, which is often brightly coloured and scented to attract insects; the androecium of stamens, each with an anther and filament, which produces pollen grains containing the male gametes; and the gynoecium of one or more carpels, each with a stigma, style and ovary containing ovules with the female gamete. A flower with both stamens and carpels is bisexual, while one with only one of them is unisexual, as in pawpaw and maize.\n\nPollination is the transfer of pollen grains from the anther to the stigma. Self pollination occurs within the same flower or between flowers of the same plant, while cross pollination occurs between two flowers of different plants of the same species and gives greater variation. Agents of pollination include insects, wind, water, birds and man. Insect pollinated flowers are large, brightly coloured, scented, produce nectar, and have sticky pollen and sticky enclosed stigmas. Wind pollinated flowers are small, dull, scentless, produce large amounts of light smooth pollen, and have long feathery exposed stigmas and pendulous anthers. Plants prevent self pollination by dioecy, protandry, protogyny, heterostyly and self sterility.\n\nAfter pollination the pollen grain germinates on the stigma and grows a pollen tube down the style, guided by chemicals, until it enters the ovule through the micropyle. Two male nuclei are released. One fuses with the egg cell to form the diploid zygote, and the other fuses with the two polar nuclei to form the triploid endosperm nucleus; this is called double fertilisation and it is unique to flowering plants. The zygote develops into the embryo with plumule, radicle and cotyledons, the ovule becomes the seed, the integuments become the testa, and the ovary wall becomes the pericarp of the fruit. Fruits and seeds are then dispersed by wind, water, animals or explosive mechanisms.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES AND EXAM STYLE QUESTIONS\n\nExample 1: State four differences between wind pollinated and insect pollinated flowers.\nAnswer: Wind pollinated flowers are small, dull coloured, scentless and nectarless with large quantities of light dry pollen and feathery exposed stigmas, while insect pollinated flowers are large, brightly coloured, scented, produce nectar, have small quantities of sticky spiny pollen and small sticky enclosed stigmas.\n\nExample 2: Toluwani grafted a scion of a high yielding orange onto a disease resistant root stock. State two advantages of this practice.\nAnswer: The desired fruit qualities of the scion are preserved exactly because no gametes are involved, and the plant benefits from the vigorous, disease resistant root system of the stock, so it matures and fruits earlier than a seedling.\n\nExample 3: Explain the fate of the following after fertilisation: ovule, ovary, integument, zygote.\nAnswer: The ovule becomes the seed, the ovary becomes the fruit, the integuments become the testa or seed coat, and the zygote develops into the embryo.\n\nExample 4: Why is cross pollination considered biologically more advantageous than self pollination?\nAnswer: Cross pollination brings together genes from two different parents, producing offspring with greater genetic variation. Such offspring are usually more vigorous, better able to survive changing environmental conditions and to resist disease, and they supply variation for natural selection and improvement of the species.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- Reproduction ensures continuity of the species.\n- Asexual reproduction involves one parent and no gamete fusion.\n- Offspring of asexual reproduction are genetically identical clones.\n- Fission occurs in bacteria and Amoeba.\n- Budding occurs in yeast and Hydra.\n- Spore formation occurs in Rhizopus and ferns.\n- Vegetative propagation uses stems, roots, leaves and buds.\n- Rhizome examples are ginger and canna.\n- Stem tuber example is Irish potato; root tuber example is cassava.\n- Bulb example is onion; corm example is cocoyam.\n- Artificial propagation includes cutting, layering, grafting and budding.\n- Grafting joins a scion to a stock.\n- Vegetative propagation preserves desirable characters.\n- Its main disadvantage is lack of genetic variation.\n- The flower is the reproductive organ of angiosperms.\n- The four whorls are calyx, corolla, androecium and gynoecium.\n- The stamen consists of anther and filament.\n- The carpel consists of stigma, style and ovary.\n- Pollen grains contain the male gametes.\n- The ovule contains the egg cell.\n- Pollination is the transfer of pollen from anther to stigma.\n- Self pollination occurs within the same plant.\n- Cross pollination occurs between different plants of the same species.\n- Protandry and protogyny prevent self pollination.\n- Dioecious plants such as pawpaw bear male and female flowers on separate plants.\n- The pollen tube enters the ovule through the micropyle.\n- Double fertilisation is unique to flowering plants.\n- The endosperm nucleus is triploid.\n- The ovary becomes the fruit and the ovule becomes the seed.\n- Dispersal agents are wind, water, animals and explosive mechanisms.\n- Dispersal reduces competition and spreads the species to new areas.',
    'source': 'AI-generated study notes',
