  {
    'subject': 'Biology',
    'chapterTitle': 'Reproduction in Animals',
    'body': 'Animals reproduce mainly sexually, by the fusion of a male gamete, the spermatozoon, with a female gamete, the ovum, to form a zygote which develops into a new individual. A few simple animals reproduce asexually, for example Amoeba by binary fission and Hydra by budding, and some insects such as aphids reproduce by parthenogenesis, in which an unfertilised egg develops into an adult.\n\nFertilisation may be external or internal. External fertilisation occurs in most fish and amphibians; gametes are shed into water, so enormous numbers must be produced because many are wasted, and there is little or no parental care. Internal fertilisation occurs in reptiles, birds and mammals; the sperm is deposited inside the female body, fewer gametes are needed, the chance of fertilisation is higher, and parental care is usually well developed. Animals may be oviparous, laying eggs that develop outside the body, as in birds and most reptiles; viviparous, giving birth to live young nourished through a placenta, as in most mammals; or ovoviviparous, in which eggs hatch inside the parent.\n\nThe human male reproductive system consists of a pair of testes housed in the scrotum outside the body, where the temperature is about two degrees lower than body temperature, which is necessary for sperm production. Each testis contains seminiferous tubules that make sperm and interstitial cells that secrete the hormone testosterone, responsible for male secondary sexual characteristics such as deep voice, beard and broad shoulders. Sperm mature in the epididymis, pass through the vas deferens and receive fluid from the seminal vesicles, prostate gland and Cowper glands to form semen, which leaves through the urethra in the penis. A sperm has a head containing the nucleus and an acrosome with enzymes, a middle piece packed with mitochondria, and a tail for swimming.\n\nThe female system consists of two ovaries, two oviducts or fallopian tubes with funnel shaped openings, the uterus with its muscular wall and lining called the endometrium, the cervix, and the vagina. From puberty, one ovum is usually released every 28 days in a process called ovulation. The menstrual cycle is controlled by four hormones: FSH from the pituitary stimulates the development of a Graafian follicle, the follicle secretes oestrogen which repairs and thickens the uterine lining, LH from the pituitary triggers ovulation at about day 14 and converts the empty follicle into the corpus luteum, and the corpus luteum secretes progesterone which maintains the thickened lining. If fertilisation does not occur the corpus luteum degenerates, progesterone falls and menstruation occurs for about five days.\n\nFertilisation normally occurs in the upper part of the oviduct. The zygote divides repeatedly as it travels to the uterus and implants in the endometrium as a blastocyst. The placenta develops and allows exchange of oxygen, dissolved food, antibodies, carbon dioxide and urea between the mother and the foetus without mixing their blood. The umbilical cord links the foetus to the placenta, and the amnion filled with amniotic fluid cushions the foetus against shock. Gestation in humans lasts about 280 days or nine months, and ends with labour, in which uterine contractions expel the baby and then the afterbirth. The newborn is nourished on milk, first as colostrum rich in antibodies.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES AND EXAM STYLE QUESTIONS\n\nExample 1: State three reasons why a fish releases millions of eggs while a human female releases one ovum a month.\nAnswer: Fertilisation in fish is external, so many gametes are lost or never meet sperm; the eggs and young are exposed to predators and unfavourable water conditions; and there is little or no parental care. In humans fertilisation is internal, the embryo develops protected within the uterus and parental care is intense, so one ovum is sufficient.\n\nExample 2: Explain why the testes are located outside the abdominal cavity.\nAnswer: The scrotum keeps the testes at a temperature about two to three degrees below normal body temperature, which is the optimum for the formation and survival of viable spermatozoa. At full body temperature sperm production is impaired.\n\nExample 3: Toluwani missed her period and a test showed a high level of progesterone. Explain the significance.\nAnswer: A sustained high level of progesterone indicates that the corpus luteum has not degenerated, which happens when implantation has occurred and the developing embryo secretes hormones that maintain it. Progesterone maintains the thickened endometrium, so menstruation does not occur; the observation suggests pregnancy.\n\nExample 4: State four functions of the placenta.\nAnswer: It allows oxygen and dissolved nutrients to pass from the mother to the foetus; it allows carbon dioxide and urea to pass from the foetus to the mother; it acts as a barrier against many harmful substances and germs and allows antibodies through; and it secretes hormones such as progesterone that maintain the pregnancy.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- Sexual reproduction involves fusion of male and female gametes.\n- Amoeba reproduces by binary fission and Hydra by budding.\n- Parthenogenesis is development of an unfertilised egg.\n- External fertilisation occurs in most fish and amphibians.\n- Internal fertilisation occurs in reptiles, birds and mammals.\n- Oviparous animals lay eggs; viviparous animals bear live young.\n- The testis produces sperm and testosterone.\n- Sperm production requires a temperature below body temperature.\n- The epididymis stores and matures sperm.\n- Semen is sperm plus fluids from accessory glands.\n- The sperm head carries the nucleus and acrosome.\n- The middle piece of the sperm is rich in mitochondria.\n- The ovary produces ova and the hormones oestrogen and progesterone.\n- Ovulation is the release of an ovum from a Graafian follicle.\n- The menstrual cycle averages 28 days.\n- FSH stimulates follicle development.\n- LH triggers ovulation at about day 14.\n- Oestrogen repairs and thickens the endometrium.\n- Progesterone maintains the endometrium.\n- The corpus luteum forms from the ruptured follicle.\n- Menstruation occurs when progesterone level falls.\n- Fertilisation occurs in the oviduct.\n- The zygote divides to form a blastocyst which implants in the uterus.\n- The placenta exchanges materials between mother and foetus without mixing blood.\n- The umbilical cord contains umbilical artery and vein.\n- Amniotic fluid protects the foetus from mechanical shock.\n- Human gestation lasts about 280 days.\n- Colostrum is the first milk and is rich in antibodies.\n- Secondary sexual characteristics appear at puberty.\n- Twins may be identical from one zygote or fraternal from two.\n- Sexually transmitted infections such as gonorrhoea and HIV can cause infertility.',
    'source': 'AI-generated study notes',
    'license': 'Internal use',
