  {
    'subject': 'Biology',
    'chapterTitle': 'Growth and Development',
    'body': 'Growth is a permanent and irreversible increase in the size, dry weight and complexity of an organism, brought about by cell division, cell enlargement and cell differentiation. It must be distinguished from a temporary increase in size such as the swelling of a seed when it absorbs water, or the bloating of a body after a meal, because these are reversible and do not involve an increase in dry matter. Development is the series of orderly changes in structure and function that accompany growth and lead to maturity.\n\nGrowth has three phases. Cell division occurs in special regions: in plants these are the meristems at the root tip, shoot tip and cambium, while in animals growth is more general but bones grow at particular growth plates. Cell enlargement follows, as the new cells absorb water, form vacuoles and elongate. Cell differentiation completes the process as the enlarged cells become specialised into tissues such as xylem, phloem, muscle or nerve.\n\nGrowth in plants is described as indeterminate or open, because meristems remain active and the plant continues to grow throughout life. Primary growth from apical meristems increases length, while secondary growth from the cambium increases girth and produces wood and bark, giving annual rings in temperate trees. Animal growth is determinate or limited; it ceases when maturity is reached. In arthropods growth is discontinuous and occurs in steps at each moult, giving a stepped growth curve.\n\nGrowth is measured by increase in length or height, by increase in fresh weight, by increase in dry weight, by increase in surface area or volume, and by counting cell number. Dry weight is the most accurate measure because it excludes variable water content, but it is destructive since the specimen must be killed and dried to constant weight. When measurements are plotted against time, the typical growth curve is sigmoid or S shaped, with a lag phase of slow initial growth, an exponential or log phase of rapid growth, a phase of decelerating growth and finally a stationary phase, sometimes followed by senescence and death.\n\nA seed germinates when it has viable embryo, water, suitable temperature and oxygen; some seeds also require light or a period of dormancy. Water softens the testa and activates enzymes, stored food is digested and translocated to the growing points, and the radicle emerges first, followed by the plumule. In epigeal germination, as in cowpea and castor oil, the hypocotyl elongates and pushes the cotyledons above the soil where they may become green. In hypogeal germination, as in maize and Indian corn, the epicotyl elongates and the cotyledon remains below the soil. Growth in animals is influenced by hormones such as growth hormone from the pituitary and thyroxine from the thyroid, and in plants by auxins, gibberellins, cytokinins, abscisic acid and ethylene. Metamorphosis, seen in insects and amphibians, is a special form of development; it may be complete, passing through egg, larva, pupa and adult as in butterflies and mosquitoes, or incomplete, passing through egg, nymph and adult as in grasshoppers and cockroaches.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES AND EXAM STYLE QUESTIONS\n\nExample 1: Why is dry weight a better measure of growth than fresh weight?\nAnswer: Fresh weight includes water, which varies greatly with time of day, watering and humidity, so it can rise or fall without real growth. Dry weight measures actual accumulated organic matter, giving a true picture of growth, although it requires killing the specimen.\n\nExample 2: Oyedele planted maize and cowpea seeds in the same box. He observed that the cotyledons of the cowpea appeared above the soil while those of maize stayed below. Name and explain the two types of germination.\nAnswer: The cowpea shows epigeal germination, in which the hypocotyl elongates and lifts the cotyledons above the soil surface. The maize shows hypogeal germination, in which the epicotyl elongates while the cotyledon remains buried in the soil.\n\nExample 3: Describe the shape of a typical growth curve and name its phases.\nAnswer: It is sigmoid or S shaped, with a lag phase of slow growth, a log or exponential phase of rapid growth, a decelerating phase and a stationary or plateau phase, which may be followed by decline in old age.\n\nExample 4: Explain why the growth curve of a cockroach is stepped rather than smooth.\nAnswer: The cockroach has a rigid chitinous exoskeleton that cannot stretch. Growth in size is possible only just after each moult, when the new cuticle is still soft and the body expands rapidly, so the curve rises steeply then flattens between moults, giving steps.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- Growth is a permanent and irreversible increase in size, dry mass and complexity.\n- Development is the orderly change in form and function toward maturity.\n- Growth involves cell division, cell enlargement and differentiation.\n- Plant growth occurs at meristems.\n- Apical meristems cause primary growth in length.\n- Cambium causes secondary growth in girth.\n- Plant growth is indeterminate; animal growth is determinate.\n- Arthropod growth is discontinuous because of moulting.\n- Growth is measured by length, fresh weight, dry weight, area or cell number.\n- Dry weight is the most accurate but destructive measure.\n- The typical growth curve is sigmoid or S shaped.\n- Phases are lag, exponential, decelerating and stationary.\n- Germination requires water, oxygen, suitable temperature and a viable embryo.\n- Water activates enzymes and softens the testa.\n- The radicle emerges before the plumule.\n- Epigeal germination brings cotyledons above the soil.\n- Hypogeal germination keeps cotyledons below the soil.\n- Cowpea and castor oil show epigeal germination.\n- Maize and Indian corn show hypogeal germination.\n- Dormancy allows seeds to survive unfavourable conditions.\n- Viability is the ability of a seed to germinate.\n- Auxin promotes cell elongation.\n- Gibberellin promotes stem elongation and seed germination.\n- Cytokinin promotes cell division.\n- Abscisic acid inhibits growth and causes leaf fall.\n- Ethylene promotes ripening of fruits.\n- Growth hormone from the pituitary controls growth in mammals.\n- Excess growth hormone causes gigantism; deficiency causes dwarfism.\n- Complete metamorphosis has egg, larva, pupa and adult stages.\n- Incomplete metamorphosis has egg, nymph and adult stages.\n- Senescence is the ageing phase that follows maturity.',
    'source': 'AI-generated study notes',
    'license': 'Internal use',
  },

  {
    'subject': 'Biology',
    'chapterTitle': 'Coordination and Control',
    'body': 'Coordination is the way the various activities of an organism are organised so that they work together smoothly and at the right time. In animals it is achieved by two systems: the nervous system, which gives rapid, short lived and localised responses using electrical impulses, and the endocrine system, which gives slower, longer lasting and widespread responses using chemical messengers called hormones carried in the blood. Plants have no nervous system and rely entirely on chemical coordination by growth substances.\n\nThe nervous system of a mammal is divided into the central nervous system, comprising the brain and spinal cord, and the peripheral nervous system, comprising the cranial and spinal nerves, together with the autonomic system that controls involuntary organs through sympathetic and parasympathetic nerves. The functional unit is the neurone, which has a cell body with nucleus, dendrites that receive impulses, and a long axon that carries impulses away, often insulated by a myelin sheath which speeds up transmission. Sensory neurones carry impulses from receptors to the central nervous system, motor neurones carry impulses to effectors such as muscles and glands, and relay or intermediate neurones connect the two within the spinal cord. Neurones do not touch; the tiny gap between them is a synapse, across which a chemical transmitter such as acetylcholine diffuses, making transmission one way.\n\nA reflex action is a rapid, automatic and involuntary response to a stimulus, such as blinking, coughing, sneezing, knee jerk or withdrawing the hand from a hot object. The pathway is the reflex arc: receptor, sensory neurone, relay neurone in the spinal cord, motor neurone, effector, response. Reflex actions protect the body and free the brain from routine tasks. A conditioned reflex is a learned response to a stimulus that would not naturally produce it, as demonstrated by Pavlov with dogs and a bell.\n\nThe brain has three main regions. The forebrain includes the cerebrum, which controls intelligence, memory, reasoning, speech and voluntary action, and the hypothalamus, which controls temperature, thirst, hunger and the pituitary. The midbrain relays visual and auditory impulses. The hindbrain includes the cerebellum, which controls balance, posture and muscular coordination, and the medulla oblongata, which controls heartbeat, breathing rate, swallowing and peristalsis. Sense organs are receptors: the eye for light, the ear for sound and balance, the nose for smell, the tongue for taste and the skin for touch, pressure, pain and temperature.\n\nThe endocrine glands are ductless and pour their hormones directly into the blood. The pituitary is the master gland and secretes growth hormone, FSH, LH, ADH, prolactin and TSH. The thyroid secretes thyroxine, which controls metabolic rate; deficiency causes goitre and cretinism, and excess causes exophthalmic goitre. The adrenal glands secrete adrenaline, the emergency hormone, which raises heart rate, breathing rate and blood glucose to prepare the body for fight or flight. The pancreas secretes insulin, which lowers blood glucose, and glucagon, which raises it; failure of insulin causes diabetes mellitus. The gonads secrete testosterone, oestrogen and progesterone.\n\nIn plants, coordination depends on hormones. Auxin, made at shoot tips, causes cell elongation and is responsible for tropisms. A tropism is a growth movement of part of a plant in response to a directional stimulus. Phototropism is response to light, and shoots are positively phototropic because auxin accumulates on the shaded side and makes it elongate faster, bending the shoot toward light. Geotropism is response to gravity; roots are positively geotropic and shoots negatively geotropic. Hydrotropism is response to water, chemotropism to chemicals, as when a pollen tube grows toward the ovule, and thigmotropism to touch, as in tendrils. Nastic movements, such as the folding of Mimosa leaves when touched, are non directional.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES AND EXAM STYLE QUESTIONS\n\nExample 1: State four differences between nervous and hormonal coordination.\nAnswer: Nervous coordination uses electrical impulses along neurones while hormonal coordination uses chemicals carried in blood; nervous responses are very fast while hormonal responses are slow; nervous effects are short lived while hormonal effects are long lasting; nervous responses are localised to specific effectors while hormonal effects may be widespread.\n\nExample 2: Toluwani touched a hot pot and withdrew her hand before she felt the pain. Explain.\nAnswer: The withdrawal is a spinal reflex. Heat receptors in the skin sent impulses along a sensory neurone to a relay neurone in the spinal cord, which passed them directly to a motor neurone supplying the arm muscle, producing withdrawal without waiting for the brain. Impulses travelling up to the cerebrum arrived slightly later, so the sensation of pain came after the movement.\n\nExample 3: Explain, in terms of auxin, why a potted plant on a windowsill bends toward the window.\nAnswer: Light coming from one side causes auxin produced at the shoot tip to migrate to the darker side. The higher auxin concentration there stimulates greater cell elongation on the shaded side than on the lit side, so the shoot curves toward the source of light. This is positive phototropism.\n\nExample 4: A student at Zetra secondary school has a swollen neck and is sluggish. Which hormone is deficient, what mineral is involved, and how can it be prevented?\nAnswer: Thyroxine is deficient, and the mineral involved is iodine. The condition, simple goitre, is prevented by using iodised salt and eating sea foods and other iodine rich foods.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- Coordination organises body activities so they work together.\n- The nervous system gives rapid, brief and localised responses.\n- The endocrine system gives slow, lasting and widespread responses.\n- The neurone is the functional unit of the nervous system.\n- Sensory neurones carry impulses to the central nervous system.\n- Motor neurones carry impulses to effectors.\n- Relay neurones connect sensory and motor neurones.\n- The myelin sheath insulates the axon and speeds transmission.\n- A synapse is the junction between two neurones.\n- Acetylcholine is a common synaptic transmitter.\n- The central nervous system is brain and spinal cord.\n- The cerebrum controls intelligence, memory and voluntary actions.\n- The cerebellum controls balance and muscular coordination.\n- The medulla oblongata controls heartbeat and breathing.\n- The hypothalamus regulates temperature, thirst and hunger.\n- A reflex action is rapid, automatic and involuntary.\n- The reflex arc runs receptor, sensory, relay, motor, effector.\n- Conditioned reflexes are learned, as shown by Pavlov.\n- Endocrine glands are ductless and secrete into the blood.\n- The pituitary is the master endocrine gland.\n- Thyroxine controls the rate of metabolism.\n- Iodine deficiency causes goitre and cretinism.\n- Adrenaline prepares the body for fight or flight.\n- Insulin lowers and glucagon raises blood glucose.\n- Diabetes mellitus results from insulin deficiency.\n- Plant hormones include auxin, gibberellin, cytokinin, abscisic acid and ethylene.\n- Tropism is a directional growth response to a stimulus.\n- Shoots are positively phototropic and negatively geotropic.\n- Roots are positively geotropic and positively hydrotropic.\n- Auxin accumulates on the shaded side of a shoot.\n- Nastic movements are non directional, as in Mimosa.\n- Synthetic auxins are used as selective weed killers and rooting powders.',
    'source': 'AI-generated study notes',
    'license': 'Internal use',
  },

  {
    'subject': 'Biology',
    'chapterTitle': 'Ecosystem and Ecological Concepts',
    'body': 'Ecology is the study of the relationships between living organisms and between organisms and their environment. An ecosystem is a natural unit made up of all the living organisms in an area, the biotic component, together with the non living surroundings, the abiotic component, interacting as a system. Examples include a pond, a forest, a grassland and a rotting log. The biosphere is the part of the earth that supports life; a biome is a large regional ecosystem such as the tropical rainforest, savanna or desert; a habitat is the particular place where an organism lives, such as the bark of a tree; and a niche is the role or function of an organism in its habitat, including what it feeds on and what feeds on it. A population is all the individuals of one species in a habitat, and a community is all the populations living together in that habitat.\n\nAbiotic factors include light, temperature, rainfall, humidity, wind, pH, salinity, soil texture and dissolved oxygen. Biotic factors include competition, predation, parasitism, symbiosis, commensalism and saprophytism. Within a community, organisms are grouped by function. Producers are green plants and other autotrophs that trap sunlight and manufacture food. Consumers are heterotrophs: primary consumers are herbivores, secondary consumers are carnivores that eat herbivores, and tertiary consumers feed on secondary consumers. Decomposers, mainly bacteria and fungi, break down dead organic matter and return mineral nutrients to the soil, thereby completing the cycles of matter.\n\nEnergy flow through an ecosystem is one way. Sunlight is trapped by producers, passed to consumers along a food chain and finally lost as heat; it cannot be recycled. A food chain is a linear sequence showing who eats whom, for example grass to grasshopper to lizard to hawk, and the arrows always point in the direction of energy flow. A food web is a network of interconnected food chains, and it represents feeding relationships more realistically. A trophic level is a feeding level in the chain. At each transfer only about ten per cent of the energy is passed on; the rest is lost in respiration, movement, excretion and as heat, and this is why food chains rarely have more than four or five links. This loss is illustrated by ecological pyramids: the pyramid of numbers, the pyramid of biomass and the pyramid of energy, of which the pyramid of energy is always upright.\n\nNutrients, unlike energy, are recycled. In the carbon cycle, carbon dioxide is removed by photosynthesis and returned by respiration, decay and combustion of fuels. In the nitrogen cycle, nitrogen fixing bacteria such as Rhizobium in root nodules and Azotobacter in soil convert atmospheric nitrogen to nitrates, lightning also fixes nitrogen, decay releases ammonia which nitrifying bacteria such as Nitrosomonas and Nitrobacter convert to nitrites and then nitrates, and denitrifying bacteria return nitrogen gas to the air. The water cycle involves evaporation, transpiration, condensation and precipitation.\n\nEcological succession is the orderly replacement of one community by another until a stable climax community is reached. Primary succession begins on bare rock or new land, starting with pioneer species such as lichens, while secondary succession follows the disturbance of an existing community, as on abandoned farmland.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES AND EXAM STYLE QUESTIONS\n\nExample 1: From the food chain maize to grasshopper to toad to snake to hawk, name the producer, the primary consumer and the tertiary consumer, and state the trophic level of the snake.\nAnswer: The producer is maize, the primary consumer is the grasshopper and the tertiary consumer is the snake. The snake occupies the fourth trophic level.\n\nExample 2: Explain why there are usually fewer hawks than grasshoppers in a grassland.\nAnswer: Energy is lost at each trophic level, with only about ten per cent transferred upward. The energy available at higher levels is therefore small and can support only a few large predators, while the abundant energy at the lower level supports many herbivores.\n\nExample 3: Oyedele grew cowpea on a plot at the Zetra farm and found the soil richer in nitrogen afterwards. Explain.\nAnswer: Cowpea is a legume whose roots bear nodules containing Rhizobium bacteria. These bacteria fix atmospheric nitrogen into nitrogenous compounds; when the roots and nodules decay, nitrates are released into the soil, enriching it.\n\nExample 4: What would happen to an ecosystem if all decomposers were removed?\nAnswer: Dead plants and animals and their wastes would accumulate, nutrients locked in dead organic matter would not be released, mineral cycles would break down, the soil would become poor in nitrates and other salts, producers would decline and the whole ecosystem would eventually collapse.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- Ecology studies organisms in relation to their environment.\n- An ecosystem consists of biotic and abiotic components interacting.\n- The biosphere is the life supporting zone of the earth.\n- A habitat is where an organism lives; a niche is its role.\n- A population is one species; a community is many populations.\n- Abiotic factors include light, temperature, rainfall, pH and salinity.\n- Biotic factors include competition, predation and symbiosis.\n- Producers are autotrophs, mainly green plants.\n- Herbivores are primary consumers.\n- Carnivores that eat herbivores are secondary consumers.\n- Decomposers include bacteria and fungi.\n- Energy flow is one way and is lost as heat.\n- Nutrients are recycled through the ecosystem.\n- A food chain shows a linear feeding relationship.\n- Arrows in a food chain show the direction of energy flow.\n- A food web is a network of interlinked food chains.\n- A trophic level is a feeding level.\n- Only about ten per cent of energy passes to the next trophic level.\n- Pyramids of numbers may be inverted; pyramids of energy are always upright.\n- Photosynthesis removes carbon dioxide from the atmosphere.\n- Respiration, decay and combustion return carbon dioxide.\n- Rhizobium in legume root nodules fixes nitrogen.\n- Nitrosomonas converts ammonia to nitrite.\n- Nitrobacter converts nitrite to nitrate.\n- Denitrifying bacteria release nitrogen gas.\n- Lightning fixes atmospheric nitrogen.\n- Succession ends in a stable climax community.\n- Lichens are common pioneer species on bare rock.\n- Symbiosis benefits both partners; parasitism harms the host.\n- Commensalism benefits one partner without harming the other.\n- Overgrazing and deforestation disrupt ecosystem balance.',
    'source': 'AI-generated study notes',
    'license': 'Internal use',
  },

