    'subject': 'Biology',
    'chapterTitle': 'Population and Community Studies',
    'body': 'A population is the total number of individuals of a particular species living in a defined area at a given time. The study of populations is important in agriculture, fisheries, public health and conservation, because it tells us whether a species is thriving, declining or dangerously overcrowded. Population size is influenced by four factors: natality, which is the birth rate; mortality, which is the death rate; immigration, the movement of individuals into the area; and emigration, the movement out of it. When natality and immigration exceed mortality and emigration, the population grows.\n\nA population growth curve is usually sigmoid. It begins with a lag phase in which few individuals are present and reproduction is slow, followed by a log or exponential phase of rapid multiplication when resources are plentiful, then a phase in which growth slows as environmental resistance builds up, and finally a stationary phase in which the population fluctuates around the carrying capacity of the environment. Carrying capacity is the maximum population that the resources of an area can support indefinitely. Environmental resistance is the sum of factors that limit growth, including shortage of food, water and space, accumulation of waste, disease, predation and competition. Density dependent factors such as disease and competition act more strongly as the population increases, while density independent factors such as drought, flood and bush fire act regardless of density.\n\nEcologists estimate population size using sampling techniques because it is rarely possible to count every individual. The quadrat method is used for plants and slow moving animals: a square frame of known area, usually one metre by one metre, is thrown at random several times, the organisms within are counted, and the mean is multiplied up to the total area. Results are expressed as density, frequency, percentage cover and abundance. The line transect and belt transect are used to study how species change along an environmental gradient, for example from the edge of a pond to dry land. For mobile animals the capture, mark, release and recapture method, known as the Lincoln index, is used: a sample is captured, marked harmlessly and released, and after time for mixing a second sample is taken. The population is estimated by multiplying the number in the first sample by the number in the second and dividing by the number of marked individuals recaptured.\n\nA community is all the populations of different species living and interacting in the same habitat. Within a community, relationships include competition, which may be intraspecific between members of the same species or interspecific between different species; predation, in which one organism kills and eats another; parasitism, in which one benefits at the expense of a host; mutualism or symbiosis, in which both benefit; commensalism, in which one benefits and the other is unaffected; and saprophytism, in which decomposers feed on dead matter. Communities show characteristic structure, dominance by certain species, stratification into layers such as the canopy, understorey and forest floor, and change over time through succession.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES AND EXAM STYLE QUESTIONS\n\nExample 1: Toluwani caught 60 tilapia in a pond, marked and released them. Two days later she caught 80 fish, of which 15 were marked. Estimate the population.\nAnswer: Population equals first sample multiplied by second sample divided by marked recaptures, that is 60 times 80 divided by 15, which equals 4800 divided by 15, giving an estimated population of 320 tilapia.\n\nExample 2: State three assumptions of the capture recapture method.\nAnswer: That the marked individuals mix randomly with the rest of the population; that marking does not harm the animal, affect its behaviour or make it more visible to predators; and that there is no significant birth, death, immigration or emigration between the two sampling occasions.\n\nExample 3: In ten quadrats each of one square metre, Oyedele counted a total of 45 Tridax plants on a field of 500 square metres. Estimate the total number.\nAnswer: The mean density is 45 divided by 10, which equals 4.5 plants per square metre. For 500 square metres the estimated total is 4.5 multiplied by 500, giving 2250 plants.\n\nExample 4: Explain why a population stops growing even when food is still available.\nAnswer: Other components of environmental resistance take effect: space becomes limited, wastes accumulate and pollute the habitat, disease spreads more easily at high density, predators are attracted, and competition for breeding sites increases. Together these raise mortality and lower natality until the population levels off at the carrying capacity.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- A population is the number of individuals of one species in an area.\n- Natality is birth rate; mortality is death rate.\n- Immigration is movement in; emigration is movement out.\n- Population density is the number per unit area.\n- Population growth curves are typically sigmoid.\n- The lag phase shows slow initial growth.\n- The log phase shows rapid exponential growth.\n- Carrying capacity is the maximum sustainable population.\n- Environmental resistance limits population growth.\n- Density dependent factors include disease and competition.\n- Density independent factors include drought and fire.\n- Quadrats are used to sample plants and slow moving animals.\n- Quadrats must be placed at random to avoid bias.\n- Results are expressed as density, frequency and percentage cover.\n- Line and belt transects show distribution along a gradient.\n- The capture recapture method suits mobile animals.\n- The Lincoln index formula is first sample times second sample divided by marked recaptures.\n- Marking must not harm the animal or increase predation risk.\n- A community is all the populations in a habitat.\n- Intraspecific competition occurs within a species.\n- Interspecific competition occurs between species.\n- Competition is keenest for food, space, light, water and mates.\n- Predation regulates prey population size.\n- Parasitism harms the host but rarely kills it quickly.\n- Mutualism benefits both partners, as in lichens.\n- Commensalism benefits one partner only.\n- Stratification is the layering of a community.\n- Dominant species exert the greatest influence in a community.\n- Overpopulation leads to famine, disease and environmental degradation.\n- Family planning and improved agriculture help manage human population pressure.\n- Ecological studies guide conservation and fisheries management.',
    'source': 'AI-generated study notes',
    'license': 'Internal use',
  },

  {
    'subject': 'Biology',
    'chapterTitle': 'Soil and Its Importance',
    'body': 'Soil is the loose upper layer of the earth crust in which plants grow. It is formed over long periods by the weathering of parent rock through physical agents such as temperature change, wind, water and ice, chemical agents such as oxidation, hydrolysis and carbonation, and biological agents such as the action of roots, burrowing animals, lichens and micro organisms. Soil is a complex mixture of five components: mineral or rock particles, humus which is decayed organic matter, soil water, soil air, and soil organisms including bacteria, fungi, earthworms, termites, nematodes and rodents.\n\nA vertical section through soil shows the soil profile, with distinct horizons. The A horizon or topsoil is dark, rich in humus and organisms and is the main zone of plant roots. The B horizon or subsoil is lighter in colour, poorer in humus and contains material leached from above. The C horizon consists of weathered parent rock fragments, and below it lies the unweathered bedrock.\n\nThree main soil types are recognised by texture, which depends on the size of the mineral particles. Sandy soil has large particles above 0.02 millimetres, is loose and gritty, well aerated, drains very quickly, has low water holding capacity and few nutrients, and warms up quickly. Clay soil has very fine particles below 0.002 millimetres, is sticky when wet and hard when dry, poorly aerated, drains very slowly, holds much water and many nutrients, and is heavy to cultivate. Loamy soil is a mixture of sand, silt and clay with a good proportion of humus; it drains well but retains adequate moisture, is well aerated and rich in nutrients, and is therefore the best soil for agriculture.\n\nHumus is extremely important. It darkens the soil so it absorbs heat, improves the crumb structure, increases water holding capacity in sandy soils, improves drainage and aeration in clay soils, releases mineral nutrients slowly as it decays, and provides food for soil organisms. Soil organisms are equally valuable: earthworms burrow and improve aeration and drainage while their casts enrich the surface, bacteria and fungi decompose dead matter and recycle nutrients, nitrogen fixing bacteria enrich the soil with nitrates, and termites mix soil layers. Some soil organisms, however, are harmful, such as nematodes and fungi that cause plant diseases.\n\nSoil pH affects the availability of nutrients; most crops grow best in slightly acidic to neutral soil of pH 6 to 7. Acid soils are corrected by liming, and alkaline soils by adding organic matter or sulphur. Soil fertility is lost through continuous cropping, leaching, erosion, burning and overgrazing, and it can be maintained by crop rotation, mixed cropping, adding organic manure and compost, applying fertilisers judiciously, mulching, cover cropping, terracing, contour ploughing, planting wind breaks and allowing land to lie fallow.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES AND EXAM STYLE QUESTIONS\n\nExample 1: Describe an experiment to compare the water retaining capacity of sandy soil and clay soil.\nAnswer: Set up two identical filter funnels plugged with cotton wool and place equal volumes of dry sandy soil and dry clay soil in them. Support each over a measuring cylinder and pour an equal known volume of water, say 100 cubic centimetres, into each. After thirty minutes measure the volume of water collected. Water retained equals water added minus water collected. The clay soil retains far more water than the sandy soil.\n\nExample 2: Why does loamy soil support crop growth better than sandy or clay soil?\nAnswer: Loam combines the advantages of both: the sand fraction gives good drainage and aeration, the clay and humus fractions retain water and nutrients, its crumb structure allows easy root penetration, and it is rich in humus and soil organisms.\n\nExample 3: A farmer supplying vegetables to Zetra market notices that his soil is becoming too acidic. State one method of correction and explain.\nAnswer: He should apply lime, that is calcium hydroxide or calcium carbonate. Lime neutralises the excess acidity, raising the pH toward neutral so that nutrients such as phosphorus become available, and it also improves the crumb structure of clay soils.\n\nExample 4: State four ways of preventing soil erosion on a sloping farm.\nAnswer: Terracing the slope, contour ploughing across the slope, planting cover crops and mulching to protect the surface, and afforestation or the planting of wind breaks and strip cropping to slow runoff.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- Soil is the loose upper layer of the earth crust that supports plant growth.\n- Soil forms by weathering of parent rock.\n- Weathering may be physical, chemical or biological.\n- Soil components are mineral particles, humus, water, air and living organisms.\n- The soil profile shows horizons A, B and C over bedrock.\n- Topsoil is richest in humus and organisms.\n- Soil texture depends on particle size.\n- Sandy soil has large particles and drains quickly.\n- Clay soil has fine particles and drains slowly.\n- Loamy soil is the best agricultural soil.\n- Humus improves structure, water holding and nutrient supply.\n- Humus darkens the soil and helps it absorb heat.\n- Earthworms improve aeration and drainage.\n- Bacteria and fungi decompose organic matter.\n- Nitrogen fixing bacteria enrich the soil with nitrates.\n- Soil air supplies oxygen for root and microbial respiration.\n- Waterlogged soil lacks oxygen and roots may die.\n- Capillarity draws water upward through soil pores.\n- Most crops prefer a pH of about 6 to 7.\n- Lime is used to correct soil acidity.\n- Leaching washes nutrients down beyond root reach.\n- Erosion removes fertile topsoil.\n- Sheet, rill and gully are types of water erosion.\n- Crop rotation restores fertility and breaks pest cycles.\n- Legumes in a rotation add nitrogen to the soil.\n- Mulching conserves moisture and suppresses weeds.\n- Fallowing allows natural recovery of fertility.\n- Organic manure improves both structure and fertility.\n- Excess inorganic fertiliser can pollute water bodies.\n- Terracing and contour ploughing control erosion on slopes.\n- Soil is a non renewable resource on the human time scale and must be conserved.',
    'source': 'AI-generated study notes',
    'license': 'Internal use',
  },

  {
    'subject': 'Biology',
    'chapterTitle': 'Water and Air Pollution',
    'body': 'Pollution is the release into the environment of substances or energy in quantities that harm living organisms or damage the quality of air, water and soil. The substances responsible are called pollutants, and they may be biodegradable, like sewage and food waste which decompose naturally, or non biodegradable, like plastics, glass, heavy metals and many pesticides which persist for very long periods and accumulate in food chains.\n\nWater pollution arises from several sources. Domestic sewage and refuse add organic matter and disease causing organisms such as those of cholera, typhoid and dysentery. Industrial effluents from factories add heavy metals such as lead, mercury and cadmium, acids, alkalis and heat. Agricultural runoff carries fertilisers and pesticides into rivers and lakes. Petroleum spillage, which is a serious problem in the Niger Delta, coats water surfaces, prevents oxygen dissolving, kills fish and destroys mangrove swamps and fishing livelihoods. Two important consequences must be understood. Eutrophication occurs when nitrate and phosphate fertilisers enrich a water body, causing algae to multiply rapidly into an algal bloom; the bloom blocks light, algae die and decomposing bacteria multiply and use up dissolved oxygen, so fish and other aquatic animals suffocate. Bioaccumulation and biomagnification occur when persistent pesticides such as DDT enter a food chain; the poison is not excreted, so its concentration increases at each trophic level and top predators such as fish eating birds are most affected.\n\nAir pollution comes from the burning of fossil fuels in vehicles, factories and generators, from bush burning, from mining and quarrying dust, and from the release of gases in industrial processes. Carbon monoxide from incomplete combustion combines irreversibly with haemoglobin to form carboxyhaemoglobin, reducing the oxygen carrying capacity of blood and causing headache, dizziness and death. Sulphur dioxide and oxides of nitrogen dissolve in rain water to form acid rain, which corrodes buildings, damages leaves, acidifies lakes and soils and kills fish. Excess carbon dioxide, methane and chlorofluorocarbons trap heat and cause the greenhouse effect and global warming, leading to melting ice caps, rising sea levels and unpredictable rainfall. Chlorofluorocarbons from aerosols and refrigerators destroy the ozone layer, allowing harmful ultraviolet radiation to reach the earth and causing skin cancer and cataracts. Smoke and soot cause respiratory diseases such as bronchitis and asthma, while noise pollution from machinery causes deafness and stress.\n\nControl measures include treating sewage and industrial effluents before discharge, enforcing environmental laws through agencies, recycling and proper disposal of waste, using unleaded fuel and catalytic converters, fitting filters and scrubbers to factory chimneys, adopting biological pest control instead of persistent pesticides, applying fertilisers in correct amounts, planting trees which absorb carbon dioxide and dust, using renewable energy such as solar power, avoiding bush burning and educating the public.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES AND EXAM STYLE QUESTIONS\n\nExample 1: Explain how the excessive use of fertiliser on a farm can kill fish in a nearby river.\nAnswer: Rain washes nitrates and phosphates into the river, enriching it. Algae multiply rapidly to form a bloom which blocks light so submerged plants die. When the algae and plants die, decomposing bacteria multiply and consume the dissolved oxygen. The biochemical oxygen demand rises, oxygen falls below the level fish need, and the fish suffocate and die. This is eutrophication.\n\nExample 2: Oyedele slept in a closed room with a petrol generator running nearby and was found unconscious. Name the pollutant and explain its effect.\nAnswer: The pollutant is carbon monoxide from incomplete combustion of fuel. It combines with haemoglobin about two hundred times more readily than oxygen to form stable carboxyhaemoglobin, so the blood cannot carry enough oxygen. The tissues, particularly the brain, are starved of oxygen, causing headache, dizziness, unconsciousness and death.\n\nExample 3: State three effects of oil spillage on an aquatic ecosystem.\nAnswer: The oil film prevents oxygen from dissolving in the water so aquatic organisms suffocate; it blocks light and reduces photosynthesis by aquatic plants and phytoplankton; it coats the gills of fish and the feathers of birds causing death; and it destroys mangrove vegetation and the livelihoods of fishermen.\n\nExample 4: A factory belonging to Zetra discharges hot water into a stream. Explain two effects.\nAnswer: This is thermal pollution. Warm water holds less dissolved oxygen, so aquatic animals may suffocate; the higher temperature also raises the metabolic rate and oxygen demand of organisms, and may kill temperature sensitive species and disrupt breeding cycles.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- Pollution is the harmful addition of substances or energy to the environment.\n- A pollutant is the substance causing pollution.\n- Biodegradable pollutants decompose naturally.\n- Non biodegradable pollutants persist and accumulate.\n- Sources of water pollution include sewage, effluents, fertilisers and oil spills.\n- Sewage introduces pathogens causing cholera and typhoid.\n- Eutrophication is nutrient enrichment leading to algal bloom.\n- Algal bloom leads to oxygen depletion and fish death.\n- Biochemical oxygen demand measures organic pollution of water.\n- Biomagnification concentrates toxins up the food chain.\n- DDT is a classic persistent pesticide.\n- Heavy metals such as mercury and lead are highly toxic.\n- Oil spillage destroys mangrove swamps and fisheries.\n- Thermal pollution reduces dissolved oxygen in water.\n- Air pollutants include carbon monoxide, sulphur dioxide and nitrogen oxides.\n- Carbon monoxide forms carboxyhaemoglobin in blood.\n- Sulphur dioxide and nitrogen oxides cause acid rain.\n- Acid rain damages crops, buildings, soils and aquatic life.\n- Carbon dioxide and methane are greenhouse gases.\n- The greenhouse effect causes global warming.\n- Global warming raises sea levels and disrupts rainfall.\n- Chlorofluorocarbons destroy the ozone layer.\n- Ozone depletion increases ultraviolet radiation and skin cancer.\n- Smoke and dust cause bronchitis and asthma.\n- Noise pollution causes deafness and stress.\n- Sewage should be treated before discharge.\n- Recycling reduces solid waste pollution.\n- Catalytic converters and unleaded fuel reduce vehicle emissions.\n- Afforestation absorbs carbon dioxide and reduces dust.\n- Biological control reduces reliance on persistent pesticides.\n- Public education and environmental laws are essential to pollution control.',
    'source': 'AI-generated study notes',
    'license': 'Internal use',
  },

  {
    'subject': 'Biology',
    'chapterTitle': 'Conservation of Natural Resources',
    'body': 'Natural resources are the materials supplied by nature that human beings use to satisfy their needs. They fall into two groups. Renewable resources, such as forests, wildlife, water, air, soil and solar energy, can replace themselves naturally if used wisely, though they can still be destroyed by over exploitation. Non renewable resources, such as petroleum, natural gas, coal, tin, columbite and other minerals, exist in fixed amounts and cannot be replaced once used up. Conservation is the careful management, protection and wise use of natural resources so that they yield the greatest benefit to the present generation while remaining available for future generations. Conservation is not the same as preservation, which means leaving a resource completely untouched.\n\nThe need for conservation arises from the rapid growth of human population, industrialisation, urbanisation and careless exploitation. Forests are destroyed by indiscriminate logging, bush burning, shifting cultivation and clearing for building; the results are soil erosion, desertification, loss of wildlife habitat, reduced rainfall and loss of medicinal plants. Wildlife is destroyed by poaching, over hunting, habitat destruction and pollution, and many Nigerian species are now endangered. Water resources suffer from pollution and wasteful use, soils from erosion, over cultivation and overgrazing, and mineral resources from wasteful extraction.\n\nMethods of conserving forests include afforestation, which is planting trees where none existed, and reafforestation, which is replanting where trees were felled; establishing forest reserves; controlled and selective felling of only mature trees; discouraging bush burning; using alternative fuels such as gas and kerosene instead of firewood; and enforcing forestry laws. Wildlife is conserved by establishing national parks such as Yankari, Kainji Lake, Gashaka Gumti and Cross River National Park, game reserves and sanctuaries; by enforcing closed hunting seasons and prohibiting the killing of endangered species; by banning trade in ivory and hides; and by public education. Soil is conserved by crop rotation, mulching, cover cropping, terracing, contour ploughing, strip cropping, planting wind breaks and controlled grazing. Water is conserved by building dams and reservoirs, treating and recycling waste water, preventing pollution and avoiding wastage. Minerals and fuels are conserved by recycling metals, avoiding wasteful mining, and developing renewable alternatives such as solar, wind and biogas energy.\n\nIn Nigeria, conservation is supported by government agencies and legislation, forestry departments, the National Environmental Standards and Regulations Enforcement Agency, and international bodies such as the World Wide Fund for Nature and the United Nations Environment Programme. Success ultimately depends on public awareness, so conservation education in schools, on radio and in communities is essential.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES AND EXAM STYLE QUESTIONS\n\nExample 1: Distinguish between renewable and non renewable resources with two examples each.\nAnswer: Renewable resources can be replaced naturally within a reasonable time, for example forests and water. Non renewable resources exist in fixed quantities and cannot be replaced once exhausted, for example petroleum and tin ore.\n\nExample 2: State four consequences of deforestation in a community.\nAnswer: Increased soil erosion and loss of fertile topsoil; reduced rainfall and possible desertification; destruction of wildlife habitats and loss of biodiversity; loss of timber, fruits, firewood and medicinal plants; and increased atmospheric carbon dioxide contributing to global warming.\n\nExample 3: Oyedele suggested that the villagers should stop hunting all animals completely. Toluwani suggested a closed season instead. Which suggestion better reflects conservation and why?\nAnswer: The closed season better reflects conservation. Conservation is wise use rather than complete non use; a closed season during the breeding period allows populations to reproduce and recover while still permitting the community to harvest a sustainable yield of bush meat afterwards.\n\nExample 4: A logging company such as Zetra fells trees in a forest reserve. State three practices it should adopt to make its operation sustainable.\nAnswer: It should practise selective felling of only mature trees, replant seedlings to replace every tree felled, and avoid clear felling on slopes and near water courses, while obeying forestry regulations and allowing felled areas to regenerate before returning.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- Natural resources are materials from nature useful to humans.\n- Renewable resources can replace themselves naturally.\n- Non renewable resources exist in fixed quantities.\n- Conservation is the wise use and management of resources.\n- Preservation means keeping a resource completely untouched.\n- Population growth increases pressure on resources.\n- Deforestation causes erosion, desertification and loss of biodiversity.\n- Afforestation is planting trees on land not previously forested.\n- Reafforestation is replanting felled forest areas.\n- Selective felling removes only mature trees.\n- Forest reserves protect timber and wildlife.\n- Bush burning destroys soil organisms and seedlings.\n- Poaching is illegal hunting of protected animals.\n- National parks in Nigeria include Yankari and Kainji Lake.\n- Game reserves and sanctuaries protect wildlife habitats.\n- Closed seasons allow animals to breed undisturbed.\n- Endangered species require legal protection.\n- Overgrazing destroys vegetation cover and hastens erosion.\n- Crop rotation and fallowing conserve soil fertility.\n- Terracing and contour ploughing control erosion.\n- Mulching conserves soil moisture.\n- Dams store water for irrigation, power and domestic use.\n- Recycling conserves metals and reduces waste.\n- Solar, wind and biogas are renewable energy sources.\n- Sustainable use means meeting present needs without harming future supply.\n- Environmental laws and agencies enforce conservation.\n- Public education is central to successful conservation.\n- Biodiversity provides food, medicine and ecological stability.\n- Wetlands and mangroves protect coasts and nurture fisheries.\n- Conservation supports tourism and national income.\n- Every citizen shares responsibility for protecting natural resources.',
    'source': 'AI-generated study notes',
    'license': 'Internal use',
  },

  {
    'subject': 'Biology',
    'chapterTitle': 'Heredity and Variation',
    'body': 'Heredity is the transmission of characteristics from parents to offspring, while variation refers to the differences that exist between individuals of the same species. Genetics is the branch of biology that studies both. The hereditary material is deoxyribonucleic acid, DNA, which is organised into genes carried on chromosomes in the nucleus. A gene is a unit of inheritance that determines a particular characteristic. Alleles are alternative forms of a gene occupying the same locus on homologous chromosomes; an organism with two identical alleles is homozygous and one with two different alleles is heterozygous. The genotype is the genetic make up while the phenotype is the observable appearance. A dominant allele expresses itself in both the homozygous and heterozygous condition, while a recessive allele shows only when homozygous.\n\nGregor Mendel, working with garden pea plants, established the basic laws of inheritance. The law of segregation states that the two alleles of a character separate during gamete formation so that each gamete carries only one of them. The law of independent assortment states that the members of one pair of alleles separate independently of the members of another pair. In a monohybrid cross between a pure breeding tall pea plant, TT, and a pure breeding dwarf, tt, all the first filial generation are Tt and tall, showing the dominance of tallness. When these are self pollinated, the second filial generation shows a phenotypic ratio of 3 tall to 1 dwarf and a genotypic ratio of 1 TT to 2 Tt to 1 tt. A test cross with the homozygous recessive is used to find out whether a dominant looking individual is homozygous or heterozygous. In incomplete dominance, as in the four o clock plant, the heterozygote shows an intermediate phenotype and the ratio becomes 1 to 2 to 1.\n\nSex determination in humans depends on the sex chromosomes. Females are XX and males are XY. All ova carry X, while sperms carry either X or Y, so the father determines the sex of the child, and there is a 50 per cent chance of either sex at each conception. Some genes are carried on the X chromosome and are said to be sex linked; examples are haemophilia and red green colour blindness, which appear far more often in males because a male has only one X chromosome and no second copy to mask the recessive allele. In the ABO blood group system, alleles A and B are codominant and both are dominant over O, giving four phenotypes.\n\nVariation may be continuous, showing a gradual range with no distinct classes, such as height, weight, skin colour and intelligence, or discontinuous, showing distinct classes with no intermediates, such as blood group, sex, tongue rolling ability and the ability to taste phenylthiocarbamide. Variation arises from meiosis through crossing over and independent assortment, from random fertilisation, and from mutations, which are sudden heritable changes in genes or chromosomes caused by radiation, chemicals or errors in replication. Sickle cell anaemia, albinism and Down syndrome are important examples. Applications of genetics include selective breeding of crops and livestock, blood transfusion matching, genetic counselling especially on the sickle cell trait, forensic identification and paternity testing.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES AND EXAM STYLE QUESTIONS\n\nExample 1: Cross a heterozygous tall pea plant with a dwarf plant and give the expected ratio.\nAnswer: Parents Tt crossed with tt. Gametes from Tt are T and t; from tt are t and t. Offspring are Tt, Tt, tt, tt. The phenotypic ratio is 1 tall to 1 dwarf, that is 50 per cent tall and 50 per cent dwarf. This is a test cross.\n\nExample 2: Oyedele has the genotype AS and his wife Toluwani is also AS. Show the possible genotypes of their children and give the probability of a child with sickle cell disease.\nAnswer: Gametes are A and S from each parent. Offspring genotypes are AA, AS, AS, SS. The ratio is 1 AA normal, 2 AS carriers with the trait, 1 SS with sickle cell disease. The probability of a child with sickle cell anaemia is one in four, that is 25 per cent, at each birth.\n\nExample 3: Explain why colour blindness is commoner in males than females.\nAnswer: The gene is recessive and carried on the X chromosome. A male has only one X chromosome, so a single recessive allele is expressed since the Y chromosome carries no corresponding allele. A female has two X chromosomes and must inherit the recessive allele from both parents before she is colour blind, which is far less likely.\n\nExample 4: State three differences between continuous and discontinuous variation.\nAnswer: Continuous variation shows a gradual range of values with intermediates, is controlled by many genes and is strongly influenced by the environment, and gives a normal distribution curve; examples are height and weight. Discontinuous variation shows distinct clear cut classes with no intermediates, is usually controlled by one or few genes and is little affected by the environment; examples are blood group and sex.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- Heredity is the transmission of characters from parents to offspring.\n- Genetics is the study of heredity and variation.\n- DNA is the hereditary material.\n- A gene is a unit of inheritance carried on a chromosome.\n- Alleles are alternative forms of a gene.\n- Homozygous means having two identical alleles.\n- Heterozygous means having two different alleles.\n- Genotype is the genetic constitution; phenotype is the appearance.\n- A dominant allele masks a recessive allele.\n- Mendel worked with the garden pea, Pisum sativum.\n- The law of segregation concerns separation of alleles at gamete formation.\n- The law of independent assortment concerns separate gene pairs.\n- A monohybrid cross involves one pair of contrasting characters.\n- The F2 phenotypic ratio of a monohybrid cross is 3 to 1.\n- The F2 genotypic ratio is 1 to 2 to 1.\n- A dihybrid cross gives an F2 ratio of 9 to 3 to 3 to 1.\n- A test cross uses the homozygous recessive parent.\n- Incomplete dominance gives an intermediate phenotype.\n- Codominance is seen in the AB blood group.\n- Females are XX and males are XY.\n- The father determines the sex of the offspring.\n- Sex linked genes are carried on the X chromosome.\n- Haemophilia and colour blindness are sex linked recessive conditions.\n- Continuous variation gives a range with intermediates.\n- Discontinuous variation gives distinct classes.\n- Mutations are sudden heritable changes in genetic material.\n- Mutagens include ultraviolet light, X rays and certain chemicals.\n- Sickle cell anaemia results from a gene mutation.\n- The AS genotype confers some resistance to malaria.\n- Down syndrome results from an extra chromosome 21.\n- Genetic counselling helps intending couples avoid genetic disease.\n- Applications of genetics include plant and animal breeding and forensic science.',
    'source': 'AI-generated study notes',
    'license': 'Internal use',
  },

  {
    'subject': 'Biology',
    'chapterTitle': 'Evolution',
    'body': 'Evolution is the gradual change in the characteristics of populations of organisms over many generations, leading to the origin of new species from pre existing ones. It explains both the unity of life, since all organisms share basic features such as cells and DNA, and its diversity, since organisms have become adapted to many different environments. The earth is estimated to be about 4.6 billion years old, and life is thought to have begun in water as simple molecules combined to form self replicating systems, an idea supported by the chemical evolution experiments of Miller and Urey.\n\nSeveral theories have been proposed. Lamarck put forward the theory of the inheritance of acquired characteristics: he argued that organs used often become better developed while unused organs degenerate, and that such acquired changes are passed to offspring. His famous example was the giraffe, said to have lengthened its neck by stretching for leaves. Lamarckism is rejected today because changes in body cells are not transmitted to gametes, as Weismann showed by cutting the tails of mice for many generations without producing tailless offspring. Charles Darwin and Alfred Russel Wallace proposed the theory of natural selection, published in 1859 in The Origin of Species. Darwin observed that organisms produce far more offspring than can survive, that populations nevertheless remain fairly constant, and that individuals in a population vary. He concluded that there is a struggle for existence in which those individuals with variations best suited to the environment survive, reproduce and pass on those favourable traits, a process he called survival of the fittest. Over long periods this changes the characteristics of the population and may produce new species. Modern or neo Darwinism combines natural selection with genetics, explaining that variation arises from mutation and recombination, and that natural selection acts on gene frequencies within populations.\n\nThe evidence for evolution comes from several fields. Fossils, the preserved remains or impressions of ancient organisms found in sedimentary rock, show a progression from simple to complex forms and provide transitional forms such as Archaeopteryx, which links reptiles and birds, and the well documented series of horse fossils. Comparative anatomy provides homologous structures, such as the pentadactyl limb of the frog, lizard, bird, bat, whale and human, which have the same basic plan but different functions and indicate common ancestry, showing divergent evolution; analogous structures such as the wings of an insect and a bird have different origins but similar functions and show convergent evolution; and vestigial structures such as the human appendix, coccyx and ear muscles are reduced remnants of once functional organs. Comparative embryology shows that vertebrate embryos are strikingly similar in early stages. Comparative biochemistry and molecular biology show similarities in DNA sequences, proteins and blood serum, with closely related species sharing more sequences. Geographical distribution, such as the differences between species on isolated islands and the Galapagos finches Darwin studied, also supports evolution.\n\nAdaptation is any feature that improves an organism chance of survival and reproduction, and it may be structural, physiological or behavioural. Modern observable examples of natural selection include industrial melanism in the peppered moth, resistance of mosquitoes to insecticides, resistance of bacteria to antibiotics, and the persistence of the sickle cell allele in malaria endemic regions.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES AND EXAM STYLE QUESTIONS\n\nExample 1: Explain the evolution of the long neck of the giraffe according to Lamarck and according to Darwin.\nAnswer: Lamarck held that ancestral giraffes stretched their necks to reach high leaves, that the necks lengthened through use, and that this acquired lengthening was inherited by the offspring. Darwin held that ancestral giraffes varied naturally in neck length; when low foliage became scarce, those with longer necks obtained more food, survived and reproduced, passing the genes for longer necks to their offspring, so that over many generations long necks became the norm.\n\nExample 2: Why do doctors warn Toluwani to complete her full course of antibiotics?\nAnswer: A bacterial population varies, and some individuals carry genes for resistance. Stopping treatment early kills only the susceptible bacteria and leaves the resistant ones alive to multiply and pass on their resistance genes. Completing the course destroys the whole population before resistant strains can be selected and spread.\n\nExample 3: Distinguish between homologous and analogous structures with one example each.\nAnswer: Homologous structures have the same basic anatomical plan and embryonic origin but perform different functions, showing common ancestry and divergent evolution; an example is the pentadactyl limb of a bat wing and a human arm. Analogous structures have different origins and structure but perform the same function, showing convergent evolution; an example is the wing of an insect and the wing of a bird.\n\nExample 4: Why does the sickle cell allele remain common in parts of Nigeria despite the death of many SS children?\nAnswer: Heterozygous individuals with the AS genotype are more resistant to falciparum malaria than normal AA individuals. In malaria endemic areas AS carriers therefore survive and reproduce more successfully, keeping the S allele at a relatively high frequency in the population. This is an example of balanced polymorphism maintained by natural selection.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- Evolution is gradual change in populations over generations.\n- Life is believed to have originated in water from simple chemicals.\n- Miller and Urey showed organic molecules can form from simple gases.\n- Lamarck proposed inheritance of acquired characteristics.\n- Lamarckism is rejected because body changes are not passed to gametes.\n- Weismann tail cutting experiment disproved Lamarckism.\n- Darwin and Wallace proposed natural selection.\n- The Origin of Species was published in 1859.\n- Organisms produce more offspring than can survive.\n- Struggle for existence results from limited resources.\n- Individuals vary within a population.\n- The best adapted survive and reproduce.\n- Survival of the fittest changes gene frequency over time.\n- Neo Darwinism combines natural selection with genetics.\n- Mutation and recombination supply variation.\n- Fossils are remains or impressions preserved in rock.\n- Palaeontology is the study of fossils.\n- Archaeopteryx links reptiles and birds.\n- Horse fossils show a well documented evolutionary series.\n- Homologous structures show divergent evolution.\n- The pentadactyl limb is the classic homologous structure.\n- Analogous structures show convergent evolution.\n- Vestigial organs include the human appendix and coccyx.\n- Vertebrate embryos are similar in early development.\n- Biochemical similarity in DNA and proteins indicates relatedness.\n- Geographical isolation can lead to formation of new species.\n- The Galapagos finches illustrate adaptive radiation.\n- Adaptation may be structural, physiological or behavioural.\n- Industrial melanism in peppered moths shows selection in action.\n- Antibiotic and insecticide resistance are modern examples of selection.\n- Sickle cell trait persists because it protects against malaria.\n- Artificial selection by man produces improved crops and livestock.',
