    'chapterTitle': 'Common Plant and Animal Pests and Diseases',
    'body': 'A pest is any organism that damages crops, livestock or stored produce, or that competes with humans for food, while a disease is a disorder that impairs the normal functioning of an organism. Together they cause enormous losses to agriculture and to human health, and their control is a major applied field of biology.\n\nCrop pests may be grouped by the way they feed and the part they attack. Biting and chewing insects include grasshoppers, locusts, caterpillars, armyworms, termites and beetles; they possess mandibles and tear leaves, stems and roots, and the swarming desert locust can strip a whole farm within hours. Piercing and sucking insects include aphids, mealy bugs, cotton stainers, capsids of cocoa and whiteflies; they insert stylets into the plant, remove sap, cause wilting and distortion, and are especially dangerous because they transmit viral diseases such as cassava mosaic and maize streak. Boring insects such as the maize stem borer and yam beetle tunnel into stems and tubers. Storage pests include weevils such as the maize weevil and cowpea weevil, rats and mould fungi, which reduce both the quantity and the quality of stored grain. Other pests are nematodes that attack roots, birds such as the weaver bird that destroy rice and millet, rodents, and monkeys.\n\nPlant diseases are caused by fungi, bacteria, viruses and nematodes. Important fungal diseases include leaf spot of groundnut, blight of cocoyam and tomato, black pod of cocoa caused by Phytophthora, and rusts and smuts of cereals. Bacterial diseases include bacterial wilt of tomato and bacterial blight of cassava. Viral diseases include cassava mosaic, maize streak and swollen shoot of cocoa, and they are spread by insect vectors and infected planting materials. Symptoms to recognise include chlorosis, wilting, mottling, leaf curling, stunting, rotting and the presence of galls.\n\nAnimals and humans also suffer from pests and diseases. Ectoparasites such as ticks, lice, fleas and mites live on the skin and suck blood, transmitting diseases; endoparasites such as tapeworm, roundworm, liver fluke and Plasmodium live inside the body. Vectors are of particular importance in Nigeria: the female Anopheles mosquito transmits malaria caused by Plasmodium; Aedes transmits yellow fever and dengue; Culex transmits filariasis; the tsetse fly Glossina transmits trypanosomiasis, which is sleeping sickness in humans and nagana in cattle; the housefly mechanically carries the organisms of cholera, typhoid and dysentery; and the blackfly Simulium transmits river blindness.\n\nControl measures fall into several groups. Cultural control includes crop rotation, early planting, clean weeding, proper spacing, destruction of crop residues, and use of resistant varieties. Physical or mechanical control includes handpicking, trapping, scaring birds, and drying and hermetic storage of grain. Chemical control uses insecticides, fungicides, nematicides and rodenticides, but these must be used carefully because of pollution, biomagnification and the development of resistance. Biological control uses natural enemies, such as ladybird beetles against aphids and parasitic wasps against borers, and is environmentally safe. Integrated pest management combines several methods for maximum effect with minimum harm. Personal and public health measures against vectors include clearing stagnant water, using treated mosquito nets, spraying, good sanitation, and prompt treatment of infected persons and animals.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES AND EXAM STYLE QUESTIONS\n\nExample 1: Oyedele found his cowpea seeds full of small round holes after three months in storage. Name the likely pest and state three control measures.\nAnswer: The pest is the cowpea weevil, Callosobruchus. Control measures include drying the seeds thoroughly before storage, storing in airtight containers or hermetic bags so that the insects suffocate, treating with approved storage chemicals or admixing with ash or vegetable oil, and removing and destroying already infested seeds.\n\nExample 2: State four ways in which insect pests damage crops.\nAnswer: They chew and defoliate leaves, reducing photosynthesis; they suck sap causing wilting and stunting; they bore into stems and tubers weakening and destroying them; they transmit viral and other diseases; and they damage stored produce, reducing its quality and market value.\n\nExample 3: Explain why biological control is often preferred to chemical control.\nAnswer: Biological control uses natural enemies of the pest, so it does not pollute the soil and water, does not kill useful organisms such as bees and earthworms, does not leave toxic residues in food, does not lead to biomagnification, and is self sustaining once the predator is established. Pests are also less likely to develop resistance.\n\nExample 4: A herd of cattle at the Zetra ranch shows fever, weakness and emaciation, and tsetse flies are common in the area. Name the disease, the causative organism and two control measures.\nAnswer: The disease is nagana, or animal trypanosomiasis, caused by Trypanosoma and transmitted by the tsetse fly, Glossina. Control measures include clearing the bush that provides breeding and resting sites for the fly, spraying insecticide, using traps and sterile male release, treating infected animals with trypanocidal drugs and rearing resistant breeds such as the Ndama.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- A pest damages crops, livestock or stored produce.\n- A disease impairs the normal functioning of an organism.\n- Biting and chewing pests include grasshoppers and locusts.\n- Piercing and sucking pests include aphids and cotton stainers.\n- Sucking pests often transmit viral diseases.\n- Boring pests include the maize stem borer and yam beetle.\n- Storage pests include weevils, rats and moulds.\n- Nematodes attack roots and cause galls.\n- Fungal diseases include black pod of cocoa and leaf spot of groundnut.\n- Bacterial diseases include bacterial wilt of tomato.\n- Viral diseases include cassava mosaic and maize streak.\n- Symptoms include chlorosis, wilting, mottling, stunting and rot.\n- Ectoparasites live on the body surface, endoparasites inside.\n- Ticks and lice are ectoparasites of livestock.\n- Tapeworm and roundworm are human endoparasites.\n- Female Anopheles mosquito transmits malaria.\n- Plasmodium is the causative organism of malaria.\n- Aedes mosquito transmits yellow fever.\n- Culex transmits filariasis.\n- Tsetse fly transmits trypanosomiasis.\n- Housefly mechanically transmits cholera and typhoid.\n- Simulium transmits river blindness.\n- Cultural control includes crop rotation and clean weeding.\n- Resistant varieties reduce losses without chemicals.\n- Physical control includes handpicking, trapping and proper drying.\n- Chemical control uses insecticides and fungicides.\n- Overuse of pesticides causes resistance and pollution.\n- Biological control uses natural enemies of pests.\n- Ladybird beetles control aphids.\n- Integrated pest management combines several control methods.\n- Sanitation, treated nets and drainage control disease vectors.\n- Quarantine prevents the spread of pests to new areas.',
