    'chapterTitle': 'Economic Importance of Living Organisms',
    'body': 'Living organisms affect human life in countless ways, some beneficial and some harmful, and the study of these effects links biology to agriculture, medicine, industry and commerce. Understanding them is essential for a student who will live in an economy that depends heavily on farming, fishing and the processing of biological materials.\n\nMicro organisms are far more useful than most people realise. Bacteria and fungi decompose dead matter and recycle nutrients, without which the soil would soon become barren. Rhizobium in the root nodules of legumes fixes atmospheric nitrogen, while Nitrosomonas and Nitrobacter convert ammonia to nitrates. Yeast, a fungus, is used in baking, where the carbon dioxide it releases makes dough rise, and in brewing and wine making, where its anaerobic respiration produces ethanol. Lactic acid bacteria are used to make yoghurt, cheese and locally fermented foods such as ogi, garri, ogiri and iru. Fungi and bacteria are the source of antibiotics: penicillin comes from the mould Penicillium and streptomycin from Streptomyces. Micro organisms also produce vitamins, enzymes used in detergents and food processing, organic acids, biogas from waste, and single cell protein, and they are used in sewage treatment and in the modern biotechnology that produces insulin and vaccines. On the harmful side, micro organisms cause food spoilage and diseases such as cholera, tuberculosis, tetanus, measles and HIV, and they spoil stored produce, leather and timber.\n\nPlants supply nearly all human food, directly as cereals, legumes, tubers, vegetables and fruits, and indirectly as feed for livestock. They provide timber, firewood, charcoal, paper, rubber, cotton, kenaf and other fibres, dyes, resins and gums. Many are medicinal: quinine from cinchona treats malaria, digitalis from foxglove treats heart conditions, aloe vera and neem, dogonyaro, are widely used in Nigeria. Plants also give shade and beauty, control erosion, act as wind breaks, absorb carbon dioxide and release oxygen, and support tourism. Harmful plants include weeds that compete with crops, parasitic plants such as mistletoe and Striga which reduce yields sharply, poisonous plants, and algal blooms that choke waterways.\n\nAnimals provide meat, milk, eggs, fish, honey, hides and skins, wool, silk and manure. Bees pollinate crops and produce honey and wax; earthworms improve soil structure; predatory insects and birds control pests naturally; livestock provide draught power and are a store of wealth. Fish farming, poultry keeping, snail rearing, rabbit keeping, bee keeping and grasscutter rearing are important sources of income and employment in Nigeria. Harmful animals include pests of crops and stores, vectors of disease such as mosquitoes and tsetse flies, parasites of humans and livestock, and predators of poultry and fish.\n\nBiology thus supports major national industries: agriculture and agro allied processing, food and beverage manufacture, pharmaceuticals, textiles, timber and paper, leather, fisheries, forestry and tourism. Careers include medicine, pharmacy, nursing, agriculture, veterinary science, biotechnology, forestry, fisheries, environmental management and teaching.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLES AND EXAM STYLE QUESTIONS\n\nExample 1: State four beneficial activities of micro organisms in agriculture.\nAnswer: They decompose dead organic matter and release nutrients into the soil; nitrogen fixing bacteria in legume nodules enrich the soil with nitrates; nitrifying bacteria convert ammonia to nitrates usable by plants; and micro organisms convert plant and animal waste into compost and biogas. Some are also used as biological control agents against crop pests.\n\nExample 2: Explain the role of yeast in bread making.\nAnswer: Yeast respires anaerobically on the sugars in the dough, producing carbon dioxide and ethanol. The carbon dioxide is trapped as bubbles in the elastic dough, making it rise and giving the bread a light, spongy texture. During baking the heat kills the yeast and evaporates the ethanol.\n\nExample 3: Zetra sets up a processing plant that needs a steady supply of cassava. State three ways in which biological knowledge would help the farmers who supply it.\nAnswer: Knowledge of plant propagation ensures the use of healthy, high yielding stem cuttings; knowledge of pests and diseases such as cassava mosaic virus and its whitefly vector allows preventive control and use of resistant varieties; knowledge of soil science guides fertiliser use, crop rotation and erosion control so that yields remain high year after year.\n\nExample 4: Give two harmful and two beneficial effects of insects to man.\nAnswer: Harmful: they destroy crops and stored produce, as with locusts and weevils, and they transmit diseases such as malaria and sleeping sickness. Beneficial: bees and butterflies pollinate crops and increase yields, honey bees produce honey and wax, silkworms produce silk, and predatory insects such as ladybirds control pests.\n\nREVISION AND SUMMARY POINTS\n\n- Living organisms may be beneficial or harmful to humans.\n- Decomposers recycle nutrients and maintain soil fertility.\n- Rhizobium fixes nitrogen in legume root nodules.\n- Nitrifying bacteria convert ammonia to nitrates.\n- Yeast is used in baking, brewing and wine making.\n- Carbon dioxide from yeast makes dough rise.\n- Lactic acid bacteria make yoghurt and cheese.\n- Local fermented foods include ogi, garri, iru and ogiri.\n- Penicillin is obtained from the mould Penicillium.\n- Streptomycin comes from Streptomyces bacteria.\n- Micro organisms are used in sewage treatment and biogas production.\n- Biotechnology uses microbes to make insulin and vaccines.\n- Harmful microbes cause food spoilage and many diseases.\n- Plants supply food, timber, fibre, rubber and medicine.\n- Quinine from cinchona is used against malaria.\n- Neem and aloe vera are common medicinal plants.\n- Plants control erosion and improve the climate.\n- Weeds compete with crops for light, water and nutrients.\n- Striga and mistletoe are parasitic plants that reduce yields.\n- Animals supply meat, milk, eggs, fish, hides and manure.\n- Bees pollinate crops and produce honey and wax.\n- Silkworms produce silk fibre.\n- Earthworms improve soil aeration and fertility.\n- Predatory insects and birds provide natural pest control.\n- Fish farming and poultry keeping create employment.\n- Snail, rabbit and grasscutter rearing are profitable micro livestock ventures.\n- Insect vectors transmit malaria, yellow fever and sleeping sickness.\n- Parasites reduce productivity of livestock.\n- Agro allied industries depend on biological raw materials.\n- Careers in biology include medicine, agriculture, pharmacy and biotechnology.\n- Sustainable use of living resources secures food supply and national income.',
